2--theory--runtime","content":"L-Leave","locale":"en"},"4818":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-68","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#table-2--theory--runtime","content":"`refresh` marking the fiber `UNLOADING`","locale":"en"},"4819":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-69","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#table-2--theory--runtime","content":"L-Unload","locale":"en"},"4820":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-70","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#table-2--theory--runtime","content":"`unload` and its inertial chaining","locale":"en"},"4821":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-71","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#table-2--theory--runtime","content":"guard","locale":"en"},"4822":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-72","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#table-2--theory--runtime","content":"`unload` awaiting notified dependents","locale":"en"},"4823":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-73","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#table-2--theory--runtime","content":"failure","locale":"en"},"4824":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-74","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#table-2--theory--runtime","content":"error recorded on the fiber, target set to `⊥`","locale":"en"},"4825":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-75","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#table-2--theory--runtime","content":"For the same mapping extended to kardia's Rust types, see the concept mapping on Development.","locale":"en"},"4826":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-76","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#effect-tracking-511-algorithm-1","content":"Every context mutation (provision, instantiation, listeners, …) reduces to one primitive, `ctx.effect`, so any operation performed through the context is automatically tracked.","locale":"en"},"4827":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-77","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#effect-tracking-511-algorithm-1","content":"`effect` accepts both effect functions and iterators (ad-hoc polymorphism); the guard is checked at each iteration boundary.","locale":"en"},"4828":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-78","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#effect-tracking-511-algorithm-1","content":"**Not checked:** the witness (that the inverse really reverts) and the coeffect commutativity witness. These are author and provider obligations.","locale":"en"},"4829":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-79","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#effect-tracking-511-algorithm-1","content":"The component level reuses `execute` with a guard testing the stability of `fiber.target`.","locale":"en"},"4830":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-80","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#coeffect-operations-512-algorithms-23","content":"Each context carries three symbol-keyed slots: `@@store` (realm symbol → value), `@@isolate` (key → realm symbol) and `@@intercept` (key → metadata).","locale":"en"},"4831":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-81","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#coeffect-operations-512-algorithms-23","content":"A binding counts as available only while the installing fiber is `ACTIVE`; a provider entering `UNLOADING` stops providing, so dependents recompute an unsatisfied target and begin their own teardown *while its bindings are still in place*.","locale":"en"},"4832":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-82","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#coeffect-operations-512-algorithms-23","content":"`isolate` and `intercept` derive a child context overriding one inherited table; recovery is implicit (discard the child). `isolate` defaults to a freshly generated symbol; `intercept` merges new metadata over the inherited value (the new value wins).","locale":"en"},"4833":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-83","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#component-lifecycle-513-algorithms-45","content":"How `refresh`, `reload` and `unload` chain into one another:","locale":"en"},"4834":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-84","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#component-lifecycle-513-algorithms-45","content":"Footnote 2 addresses Rust explicitly: *\"with lazy scheduling (e.g. Python coroutines, Rust futures) the host must spawn the task for it to progress\"*. The algorithms write `create_task` out for language independence.","locale":"en"},"4835":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-85","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#component-lifecycle-513-algorithms-45","content":"**In kardia:** transitions and asynchronous effect bodies are spawned as tokio tasks, so they progress without being polled by the caller, and they are never dropped half-way. All lifecycle bookkeeping happens in short critical sections under one state lock per root; no user code runs while the lock is held. See Concurrency.","locale":"en"},"4836":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-86","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#component-lifecycle-513-algorithms-45","content":"`fiber.target` is a digest tupling the uid of each declared key's provider. A **uid is never reused**, so a replaced provider is never mistaken for the old one even if the values are equal. Consequently a provider overwriting its own binding *in place* is not observed: to propagate a replacement, withdraw and re-install.","locale":"en"},"4837":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-87","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#component-lifecycle-513-algorithms-45","content":"There are two levels of checking: the transition level (`reload`/`unload` check the target at completion, giving inertial chaining) and the iteration level (`execute` checks at every boundary, giving partial rollback).","locale":"en"},"4838":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-88","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#component-lifecycle-513-algorithms-45","content":"Three lines carry Theorem 70: commit at reload start, and discard the committed view only after every inverse; mark `UNLOADING` before scheduling the task; `unload` waits for each notified dependent. The wait sits **before** the whole recovery, since `fiber.dispose` initiates a fiber's effects concurrently.","locale":"en"},"4839":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-89","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#component-lifecycle-513-algorithms-45","content":"Termination follows Theorem 73 (Metatheory): the provider graph is traversed on demand.","locale":"en"},"4840":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-90","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#context-access-514-algorithm-6","content":"Besides reflective `ctx.get`/`ctx.set`, coeffects are read as **properties** (`ctx[key]`) through a JavaScript `Proxy`:","locale":"en"},"4841":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-91","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#context-access-514-algorithm-6","content":"Unlike `ctx.get` (which never fails), the proxy resolves against the accessing fiber's **committed view** and enforces the specification at the point of use. Reading the view rather than the store is what keeps a dependency readable during the consumer's own teardown (Theorem 70). The check is made at runtime but, since `inject` is static, could be made at compile time (§6.4, for example with Rust procedural macros or traits; see Discussion and related work).","locale":"en"},"4842":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-92","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#context-access-514-algorithm-6","content":"**In kardia:** there is no proxy. `ctx.get::<S>()` performs Algorithm 6 against committed views and fails with `AccessError::Undeclared`, `AccessError::Inactive` or `AccessError::TypeMismatch`; `ctx.live::<S>()` is the reflective read of the live store, which never fails for an undeclared key. The compile-time check is there too: a `#[plugin]` function taking `Injected<(A, B)>` declares `A` and `B`, and `get::<S, _>()` on it does not compile for an undeclared `S`. See Services and Plugins.","locale":"en"},"4843":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-93","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"**Definition 81 – entry:** `id` (stable reconciliation key), `url` (component module), `isolate`, `intercept`, `config`, `disabled`. An entry is a faithful specification because it records exactly what the support set reads: `disabled` → `τ`, tree parent → `π`, `url` → `(d, p)`. Entries form a **configuration tree**; `@cordisjs/group` loads a list of child entries; `@cordisjs/include` grafts an external YAML or JSON file. Binding is **bidirectional**: a component that revises its own config or disables itself has the change written back.","locale":"en"},"4844":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-94","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"**Reconciliation** is incremental, justified by Theorem 80 (the endpoint is a function of the final config), Theorem 73 (it quiesces), Corollary 69 (rebuilding one entry leaves its neighbours untouched) and Theorem 70 (entries can be instantiated together, and a fiber waits at L-Begin, so **modules are loaded concurrently**). Changes are dispatched per field:","locale":"en"},"4845":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-95","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"Field changed","locale":"en"},"4846":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-96","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"Action","locale":"en"},"4847":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-97","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"`id`, `url`","locale":"en"},"4848":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-98","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"rebuild the entry","locale":"en"},"4849":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-99","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"`isolate`","locale":"en"},"4850":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-100","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"reassign realms (Algorithm 7)","locale":"en"},"4851":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-101","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"`intercept`","locale":"en"},"4852":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-102","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"update in place (consulted at read time; no reload)","locale":"en"},"4853":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-103","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"`config`","locale":"en"},"4854":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-104","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"hand to the component, which typically diffs and reloads only on a material change. A group's config is its child list, so it gets a keyed diff over child ids, recursing","locale":"en"},"4855":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-105","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"`disabled`","locale":"en"},"4856":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-106","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"unload when set, reload when cleared","locale":"en"},"4857":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-107","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"**Managed realms.** `isolate: true` gives a *local* realm private to the entry, tagged by its id and carried along when the entry moves; `isolate: \"name\"` gives a *global* realm shared by every entry naming that string. A realm is discarded once no entry names it.","locale":"en"},"4858":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-108","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"**Algorithm 7 – realm reassignment with delimiters.** There is one delimiter symbol `δ_k` per key; each context stores a tag under `δ_k`, inherited by descendants and redrawn on each reassignment, so that `γ′[δ_k] = d₁ ⇔ γ′` derives from the entry's context.","locale":"en"},"4859":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-109","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"A dependent gains or loses the binding exactly when the move separates it from the provider.","locale":"en"},"4860":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-110","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#declarative-configuration-521","content":"**In kardia:** the declarative loader is the `kardia-loader` crate (entries, groups, realms and a plugin `Catalog`), and file-backed include with two-way sync is `kardia-include`. See Loader and Loader internals.","locale":"en"},"4861":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-111","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#hot-module-replacement-522","content":"Because a fiber bounds all of a component's effects, a module that is a component can be replaced through fiber operations alone, with **no developer-annotated acceptance boundaries** (unlike Webpack or Vite).","locale":"en"},"4862":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-112","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#hot-module-replacement-522","content":"**Phase 1 – classification (Algorithm 8).** The inputs are `stashed` (the changed files) and `externals` (non-replaceable modules, whose change needs a full restart). A fixed point over `get_imports`: a pending module is *accepted* if one of its imports is accepted, and *declined* if all its imports are declined; leftovers (cycles) default to declined.","locale":"en"},"4863":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-113","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#hot-module-replacement-522","content":"**Phase 2 – stale-entry detection (Algorithm 9).** For each entry, collect the transitive imports of `entry.url`, stopping at declined modules; if the tree meets `accepted`, the entry is stale and its tree is folded into `accepted`.","locale":"en"},"4864":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-114","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#hot-module-replacement-522","content":"**Phase 3 – transactional reload (Algorithm 10).** Invalidate the caches of accepted modules (backing them up), re-import each stale entry and swap in a fresh fiber; on any error, restore the caches and rebuild every stale entry from the backup, then rethrow. The system never enters a half-reloaded state. (On Node.js both the ESM and the CJS caches are cleared.)","locale":"en"},"4865":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-115","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#hot-module-replacement-522","content":"**In kardia:** Rust has no module registry, so `kardia-hmr` keeps its own `ModuleGraph`, loaded from a `ModuleSource` (WebAssembly, dynamic libraries or child processes), and replaces the plugins a changed module exports through `registry.replace`. Changed host modules are reported for a restart instead of being applied. A failed import aborts only its own unit (the plugins whose modules it shares), whose cache entries are restored, while the other units are applied. See Hot reload and Loader internals.","locale":"en"},"4866":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-116","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#case-study-koishi-53","content":"Koishi is a chatbot framework, 4+ years old, with more than 4000 community plugins (IM adapters, database drivers, consoles, features); its web console is a second, independent Cordis application in the browser. The model is therefore **expressive** (sufficient for a production system) and **general** (no domain or runtime presupposed).","locale":"en"},"4867":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-117","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#case-study-koishi-53","content":"Disabling a plugin from the console reverts its effects in place; HMR re-applies edited plugins on save while preserving caches and connections elsewhere. Authors get ordered cleanup without writing an uninstall path.","locale":"en"},"4868":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-118","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#case-study-koishi-53","content":"There is genuine dependency topology (adapters, databases, features). Reconfiguring a provider reactivates only the dependents whose resolved dependency changed; a plugin with an unavailable dependency waits without erroring.","locale":"en"},"4869":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation-119","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-implementation#case-study-koishi-53","content":"**Threats to validity:** a single ecosystem, a single language, observational evidence; measuring overhead and productivity is future work.","locale":"en"},"4870":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","type":"page","content":"Metatheory and extensions","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","locale":"en"},"4871":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-0","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","content":"Paper §4.3–4.4: preservation, recovery exactness, ordering, resolution coherence, progress and confluence, and the extensions for asynchrony, failure, isolation and configuration.","locale":"en"},"4872":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-1","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#setup-43-definition-58","content":"Setup (§4.3, Definition 58)","locale":"en"},"4873":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-2","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#preservation-431","content":"Preservation (§4.3.1)","locale":"en"},"4874":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-3","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#temporal-composability-global-form-432","content":"Temporal composability, global form (§4.3.2)","locale":"en"},"4875":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-4","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#spatial-composability-global-form-433","content":"Spatial composability, global form (§4.3.3)","locale":"en"},"4876":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-5","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#progress-434","content":"Progress (§4.3.4)","locale":"en"},"4877":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-6","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#confluence-435","content":"Confluence (§4.3.5)","locale":"en"},"4878":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-7","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#extensions-44","content":"Extensions (§4.4)","locale":"en"},"4879":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-8","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#asynchrony-and-inertia","content":"Asynchrony and inertia","locale":"en"},"4880":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-9","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#failure","content":"Failure","locale":"en"},"4881":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-10","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#isolation","content":"Isolation","locale":"en"},"4882":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-11","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#configuration","content":"Configuration","locale":"en"},"4883":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-12","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Obligations the theory places on an implementation","locale":"en"},"4884":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-13","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","content":"This page summarises §4.3–4.4 of the paper (arXiv:2608.25512). Each theorem is stated informally, with the practical guarantee it buys and the implementation obligation it implies. The rules it reasons about are on the calculus page.","locale":"en"},"4885":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-14","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","content":"**In kardia:** several of these theorems are property tests. `crates/kardia-core/tests/model.rs` runs random orchestration sequences of synthetic components (writes to a shared table, each with an exact inverse) under real tokio schedules with random delays. It checks ordering (Theorem 70) during every run, and preservation (Theorem 64), recovery exactness (Theorem 68 / Corollary 69) and confluence (the Active fibers are exactly the support set, Theorem 80) at quiescence. See Testing.","locale":"en"},"4886":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-15","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#setup-43-definition-58","content":"Steps are indexed by `t`; `step^t = r(n)` is the rule applied and the fiber it acts on. Runs start from an empty registry. An **episode** of `n` is a maximal interval during which `installed_n` holds; it opens just after an L-Begin and closes at an L-Unload. Every step factors as `γ^{t+1} = edit^t(Ψ^t(γ^t))`; tables (`σ_m`) move only inside `Ψ`, control fields (`θ, τ, π, d, p, e`, the registry domain) only inside `edit` (except through instantiation). States are compared with a `≃` that keeps all control fields (eq. 54).","locale":"en"},"4887":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-16","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#setup-43-definition-58","content":"**Lemma 59 (lookups in Table 1):**","locale":"en"},"4888":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-17","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#setup-43-definition-58","content":"a table moves only inside a `Ψ` of its own fiber or of a fiber declaring keys it holds (values only, domain unchanged);","locale":"en"},"4889":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-18","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#setup-43-definition-58","content":"`ω_n` is created only by L-Begin and destroyed only by L-Unload, so it is **constant over an episode**;","locale":"en"},"4890":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-19","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#setup-43-definition-58","content":"only L-Unload(n) applies `g_n`;","locale":"en"},"4891":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-20","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#setup-43-definition-58","content":"episodes open by L-Begin and close by L-Unload;","locale":"en"},"4892":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-21","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#setup-43-definition-58","content":"`π, d, p, e` are written once at insertion; `τ` is monotone (only O-Retire writes it, and only to `⊤`).","locale":"en"},"4893":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-22","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#setup-43-definition-58","content":"**Lemma 60 (≃-invariance)**: the rules cannot distinguish `≃`-related states. **Lemma 61 (equivariance)**: results hold up to renaming fiber names. **Lemma 62 (vestigial entries)**: a retired, Inactive, empty, childless entry is invisible to all rules.","locale":"en"},"4894":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-23","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#preservation-431","content":"**Definition 63 – well-formed registry:** (1) parents exist (or are `root`); (2) provisions are pairwise disjoint; (3) an installed fiber's `ω` is total on `d_n` and names existing fibers; (4) every fiber an installed `ω` names is installed.","locale":"en"},"4895":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-24","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#preservation-431","content":"**Theorem 64**: every rule preserves well-formedness. The guard on L-Unload is what carries clauses (3) and (4): no committed view ever names a removed fiber. Consequences: a name freed by O-Remove may be reissued (there are no stale views), and a fiber can be removed as soon as it is Inactive without a separate dependency check.","locale":"en"},"4896":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-25","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#temporal-composability-global-form-432","content":"**Definition 65**: fibers `m, n` are *entangled* when one's provision meets the other's declarations. A run is *pairwise independent* when every pair of fibers is independent, or entangled with only commutative shared keys.","locale":"en"},"4897":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-26","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#temporal-composability-global-form-432","content":"**Lemma 66**: every run is pairwise independent (supplied by witnessed coeffects and Theorem 47, see The context paradigm).","locale":"en"},"4898":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-27","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#temporal-composability-global-form-432","content":"**Lemma 67 (entangled steps)**: while `n`'s episode is open, a provider `m` of `n` takes only identity state maps (the guard blocks its L-Unload); a consumer's steps commute with `g_n`; while `n` is Reloading, entangled fibers take only identity maps.","locale":"en"},"4899":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-28","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#temporal-composability-global-form-432","content":"**Theorem 68 (Recovery exactness)**: applying `n`'s accumulator at any point of its episode leaves every table where the *other* fibers' steps alone would have left it from the episode start:","locale":"en"},"4900":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-29","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#temporal-composability-global-form-432","content":"*Running an inverse withdraws the fiber's contribution and nothing else.*","locale":"en"},"4901":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-30","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#temporal-composability-global-form-432","content":"**Corollary 69 (Terminal recovery)**: after an episode closes, the state equals \"other fibers only\"; in particular `σ_n = ∅` (which is O-Remove's premise).","locale":"en"},"4902":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-31","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#temporal-composability-global-form-432","content":"*Bound:* tables are restored only up to each key's `≃_k`: a monotone allocator is not rewound, heap layout is not restored, a sent message stays sent.","locale":"en"},"4903":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-32","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#spatial-composability-global-form-433","content":"**Theorem 70 (Ordering)**","locale":"en"},"4904":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-33","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#spatial-composability-global-form-433","content":"A fiber begins a transition only where its dependencies are provided (`L-Begin(m) ⇒ γ ⊧ d_m`).","locale":"en"},"4905":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-34","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#spatial-composability-global-form-433","content":"If `m` committed key `k` to provider `n`, then throughout `m`'s episode: `ω_m(k) = n`; `n`'s episode strictly contains `m`'s (`b < b′`, and `u′ < u` if `n` closes), so **a provider outlives its consumers**; the binding `k` stays in `σ_n` and moves only by operations at `k` of fibers declaring `k`.","locale":"en"},"4906":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-35","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#spatial-composability-global-form-433","content":"**Theorem 71 (Resolution coherence)**: every iteration of a transition runs against the one committed resolution `ω`. When the fiber leaves Reloading, either L-Finish lands it Active against `ω`, or L-Divert takes it out and its episode closes with exact recovery. A transition never straddles two resolutions of its coeffects (no glitch *within* a transition; Cordis has no global \"turn\" as FRP has).","locale":"en"},"4907":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-36","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#progress-434","content":"**Definition 72**: precedence `n ≺ m := p_n ∩ d_m ≠ ∅` (`n` may provide a key `m` declares). Acyclicity of `≺` is an **assumption** (a component that declares a key it provides itself violates it).","locale":"en"},"4908":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-37","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#progress-434","content":"**Theorem 73 (Progress)**: assume `≺` is acyclic, iterator length is at most `K`, and there are finitely many names. Then:","locale":"en"},"4909":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-38","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#progress-434","content":"**No deadlock**: a non-quiescent state always admits some lifecycle rule (the chain of Unloading fibers blocked by relied-upon consumers is `≺`-increasing, hence finite, and ends at an unblocked one).","locale":"en"},"4910":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-39","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#progress-434","content":"**Termination**: each fiber takes at most `(K + 3)(V(n) + 1)` steps, where `V(n)`, the number of target turns, is finite.","locale":"en"},"4911":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-40","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#progress-434","content":"Every maximal sequence of lifecycle steps ends quiescent. Finiteness of names requires that no component instantiates itself without bound.","locale":"en"},"4912":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-41","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#confluence-435","content":"**Definition 74 – support set A**: `n ∈ A ⇔ ¬τ_n ∧ (π_n = root ∨ π_n ∈ A) ∧ ∀k ∈ d_n. ∃m ∈ A. k ∈ p_m`, a function of `τ, π, d, p` only. **Lemma 75**: the support relation is well-founded and `A` is unique.","locale":"en"},"4913":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-42","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#confluence-435","content":"**Definition 76 – total on its provision**: a finished activation has installed every key of `p`. **Lemma 77**: at quiescence (with totality), `A` is the set of Active fibers.","locale":"en"},"4914":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-43","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#confluence-435","content":"**Lemma 78 (Transposition)** and **Lemma 79 (Deletion)**: adjacent independent steps can be swapped, and a closed episode (with its instantiations) can be deleted without changing the endpoint.","locale":"en"},"4915":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-44","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#confluence-435","content":"**Theorem 80 (Confluence)**: any run reaching a quiescent state can be reduced to a **canonical** run: the same orchestration steps, then one episode per supported fiber in an order linearising support. Two runs with the same orchestration inputs reach `≃`-related states (up to renaming). *The lifecycle relation has unique normal forms.*","locale":"en"},"4916":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-45","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#confluence-435","content":"> \"The theorem is what licenses reasoning about a Cordis application as though it were statically assembled.\"","locale":"en"},"4917":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-46","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#confluence-435","content":"Adding, removing, replacing and un-replacing components arrives at the state of writing the final composition down at the outset. The theorem speaks about *state*, not about emissions made along the way.","locale":"en"},"4918":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-47","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#asynchrony-and-inertia","content":"In an async host, iterations and inverses are futures that run to completion once started, so the **aborting** alternative of L-Divert is unavailable. Such a host is **inertial**: a fiber whose target turns during an iteration deactivates after that iteration lands. All results quantify over all runs, so they cover inertial runs; Theorem 73 never uses the aborting alternative. Inverses in flight need no counterpart (Unloading is never declined). A deactivation may chain directly back into an activation (implementation: `reload`/`unload` mutual chaining, see Implementation in the paper).","locale":"en"},"4919":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-48","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#asynchrony-and-inertia","content":"**In kardia:** transitions are inertial. An effect body or a plugin's `apply` is spawned and never dropped half-way; disposal waits for it to reach its end or an iteration boundary (`ctx.checkpoint()`, `scope.checkpoint()`, `scope.yield_(..)`), where it can stop with `Interrupted`. See Concurrency and Lifecycle.","locale":"en"},"4920":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-49","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#failure","content":"An iteration may raise: `Γ → Either(Ξ, Γ × (Γ→Γ) × Maybe(ℑ))`. A raise exits Reloading like an aborting L-Divert (chosen by the iteration, not the environment): the fiber unwinds through Unloading with the accumulator built so far, arrives Inactive having installed nothing, and the **error is recorded on the fiber**. L-Begin then requires an error-free fiber: a failed effect is *not retried against an unchanged environment*; `quiet` admits failed fibers; failure does not propagate to the parent (siblings keep running). A retry is a revision (re-insertion without the outcome). Confluence excludes failed fibers. In the implementation this is the state `FAILED`.","locale":"en"},"4921":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-50","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#failure","content":"`Fiber::set_recovery` now selects `Manual` (default), bounded `Retry`, or `Host` handling. The supervisor outlives the failed instance, waits for cleanup and cancels on disposal or policy changes. Loader entries expose the same choice through `recovery`; see configuration and examples and native fault evidence. `restart()` still does not clear failure; manual handling uses `update`, while selected supervision drives the supported recovery operation. Unrelated plugins must remain operational under every policy.","locale":"en"},"4922":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-51","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#isolation","content":"With realms fixed at insertion, the relaxed calculus is the present one over keys `K × R`: a fiber under realm table `ρ` declares and provides `(k, ρ(k))`; disjointness becomes \"disjoint within a realm\". Reassigning a realm at runtime changes a fiber's interface, so it is handled as a *revision*. Interception needs no extension: metadata is consulted at access time and is in no fiber field.","locale":"en"},"4923":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-52","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#configuration","content":"A component takes a configuration bound into its effect function; one component with two payloads is two components. The declarative layer may **revise** a running fiber (replace its config, reassign realms, disable or re-enable it). Disabling is O-Retire; every other revision is retire → deactivate → remove (children first) → reinsert at the same name. Re-enabling uses this composite rather than resetting `τ` to `⊥` (the monotonicity of `τ` underlies Theorem 80, and a re-enabled orphan would activate with its creator gone). In the implementation, re-enabling an *entry* instantiates a *fresh fiber*: the entry is the identity across revisions, the fiber the identity of one enablement. The shortcuts the loader takes (config handed to the component, a realm moved without reloading the provider) are justified by their *endpoint* (Theorem 80).","locale":"en"},"4924":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-53","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Obligation","locale":"en"},"4925":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-54","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Source","locale":"en"},"4926":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-55","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Disposers must revert exactly their effect (author obligation, unchecked)","locale":"en"},"4927":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-56","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Def. 8 witness","locale":"en"},"4928":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-57","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Shared keys must be commutative (provider obligation, unchecked)","locale":"en"},"4929":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-58","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Def. 46","locale":"en"},"4930":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-59","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Single provider per (key, realm)","locale":"en"},"4931":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-60","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"O-Insert premise","locale":"en"},"4932":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-61","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Provider available only while Active","locale":"en"},"4933":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-62","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"eq. 46","locale":"en"},"4934":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-63","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Committed view fixed per episode, read by consumers","locale":"en"},"4935":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-64","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Lemma 59(2), Thm 70","locale":"en"},"4936":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-65","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Out-of-service mark before any inverse runs","locale":"en"},"4937":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-66","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"L-Leave","locale":"en"},"4938":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-67","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Unload waits for dependents (guard)","locale":"en"},"4939":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-68","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"L-Unload","locale":"en"},"4940":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-69","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Iteration-boundary staleness check","locale":"en"},"4941":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-70","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"L-Iter/L-Finish premise, Thm 71","locale":"en"},"4942":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-71","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Failed fiber not retried until revised","locale":"en"},"4943":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-72","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"§4.4 Failure","locale":"en"},"4944":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-73","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Report dependency cycles (`≺` not acyclic ⇒ permanently inactive)","locale":"en"},"4945":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-74","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"Thm 73 assumption, §6.5 (Discussion)","locale":"en"},"4946":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory-75","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-metatheory#obligations-the-theory-places-on-an-implementation","content":"**In kardia:** dependency cycles are reported when a fiber is inserted. The registry searches the declared `provide`/`inject` of live fibers for a cycle through the new fiber and logs a warning naming it (`dependency cycle: a -> b -> a`); the fiber stays `Pending`. Declared `provide` is metadata only, so a plugin that provides undeclared keys escapes the check. See Lifecycle.","locale":"en"},"4947":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","type":"page","content":"Reactive coeffects","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","locale":"en"},"4948":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-0","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","content":"Paper §3.2: the coeffect context, specifications and notification, isolation and interception, which give a single component local spatial composability.","locale":"en"},"4949":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-1","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#coeffect-context-321","content":"Coeffect context (§3.2.1)","locale":"en"},"4950":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-2","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#definition-19--coeffect-context","content":"Definition 19 – coeffect context","locale":"en"},"4951":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-3","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#definition-20--get--set","content":"Definition 20 – get / set","locale":"en"},"4952":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-4","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#specification-and-notification-322","content":"Specification and notification (§3.2.2)","locale":"en"},"4953":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-5","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#definition-21--coeffect-specification","content":"Definition 21 – coeffect specification","locale":"en"},"4954":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-6","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#definition-22--notification","content":"Definition 22 – notification","locale":"en"},"4955":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-7","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#isolation-and-interception-323","content":"Isolation and interception (§3.2.3)","locale":"en"},"4956":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-8","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#realization-definition-23","content":"Realization (Definition 23)","locale":"en"},"4957":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-9","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#coeffect-isolation--definitions-24-25","content":"Coeffect isolation – Definitions 24, 25","locale":"en"},"4958":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-10","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#coeffect-interception--definitions-26-27","content":"Coeffect interception – Definitions 26, 27","locale":"en"},"4959":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-11","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Engineering take-aways","locale":"en"},"4960":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-12","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","content":"This page summarises §3.2 of the paper (arXiv:2608.25512). Definition numbers are the paper's. For the Cordis realisation see Context and reflection and Fiber lifecycle in the Cordis reference.","locale":"en"},"4961":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-13","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","content":"Spatial composability is the ability of components to declare dependencies and of the system to resolve, provide and withdraw them at runtime. Dependency satisfaction must be re-evaluated whenever the shared context changes, so that a component activates when its dependencies become available and deactivates when they are withdrawn.","locale":"en"},"4962":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-14","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#coeffect-context-321","content":"The paper formalises an inversion-of-control (IoC) container as a coeffect context.","locale":"en"},"4963":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-15","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#definition-19--coeffect-context","content":"For a type family `𝒱 : K → Type`, `Σ := (k : K) ⇀ 𝒱_k` is a **finite dependent partial function**. Notation: `σ(k)` application, `σ[k ↦ v]` extension, `σ ∖ k` restriction, `k ∈ dom(σ)` membership. The type family gives each key a static value type.","locale":"en"},"4964":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-16","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#definition-19--coeffect-context","content":"**Preconditions:** a dependency may not be provided twice (`k ∉ dom(σ)` for extension) nor revoked if absent (`k ∈ dom(σ)` for restriction). A violated precondition is *signalled as an error and produces no transition* (equivalently, compose in the `Maybe` monad).","locale":"en"},"4965":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-17","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#definition-20--get--set","content":"`set(k, v)` **is an effect function** (`𝔈Σ*`). The effect machinery of §3.1 (Revertible effects) therefore applies directly: dependency registrations are tracked and recovered automatically. *\"Coeffect operations are effects, and effects are revertible.\"*","locale":"en"},"4966":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-18","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#specification-and-notification-322","content":"Accessing an absent dependency is a runtime failure, so a component should **activate only once all its declared dependencies are present**, instead of accessing them optimistically.","locale":"en"},"4967":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-19","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#specification-and-notification-322","content":"**Satisfaction predicate**: `σ ⊧ d := ∀k ∈ d. k ∈ dom(σ)`, decidable because `dom(σ)` is finite.","locale":"en"},"4968":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-20","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#specification-and-notification-322","content":"Because every mutation of `σ` passes through effect functions, changes of satisfaction are detectable at each effect boundary: *the effect system guarantees that every coeffect change is observed.*","locale":"en"},"4969":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-21","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#definition-21--coeffect-specification","content":"`𝔇Σ := Set(K)`.","locale":"en"},"4970":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-22","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#definition-22--notification","content":"An activating transition triggers execution of the component's effects (tracked as in §3.1); a deactivating one triggers recovery through the accumulator.","locale":"en"},"4971":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-23","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#definition-22--notification","content":"**Local spatial composability**: a component activates only at a state satisfying its specification (it never reads an absent binding), and every context change is classified against the specification (loss of satisfaction is detected where it happens). Two things are left to the global form (§4.3.3, see Metatheory): withdrawing a binding only *after* the deactivations it causes have finished, and keeping the bindings an activation reads unmoved while it runs.","locale":"en"},"4972":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-24","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#isolation-and-interception-323","content":"The flat table cannot bind *different* values to the same logical dependency for different components. The paper adds two extensions.","locale":"en"},"4973":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-25","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#realization-definition-23","content":"An effect function on a context admits two realizations:","locale":"en"},"4974":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-26","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#realization-definition-23","content":"**In-place**: mutate the context and return a non-trivial inverse; recovery runs the inverse.","locale":"en"},"4975":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-27","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#realization-definition-23","content":"**Derived**: leave the input intact and return a *fresh context deriving from it* with the identity inverse; recovery discards the derived context.","locale":"en"},"4976":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-28","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#realization-definition-23","content":"In a purely functional setting the two coincide; an imperative host may choose per operation. **Isolation and interception are given derived realization**: they produce a child context whose own table differs from the inherited one, leaving the shared table untouched, so there is nothing to track. Assignment on a derived table overrides the inherited one at that key, so neither operation has a precondition.","locale":"en"},"4977":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-29","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#coeffect-isolation--definitions-24-25","content":"The two-layer resolution `k → ρ(k) → σ(ρ(k))` decouples the logical layer from storage. The paper describes this as **runtime ad-hoc polymorphism**: the same key resolves to different values in different contexts, adjustable at runtime (multi-tenant systems, testing, sandboxes). `set` remains an effect function (revertible); `isolate` needs no inverse.","locale":"en"},"4978":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-30","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#coeffect-interception--definitions-26-27","content":"Interception attaches cross-cutting **metadata** to dependency access without changing the value.","locale":"en"},"4979":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-31","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#coeffect-interception--definitions-26-27","content":"`ι` is the **context-carried** metadata (installed by an enclosing context); `σ(k)` is a *provider function* from metadata to value; the specification `d(k)` is the **component-declared** metadata.","locale":"en"},"4980":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-32","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#coeffect-interception--definitions-26-27","content":"On access the system evaluates `σ(k)(d(k) ⊕_k ι(k))`. The merge follows each key's semantics (scalars overwritten, sets unioned) and is **right-biased**: context-carried metadata wins, so an enclosing context can constrain how a component uses a coeffect without modifying the component (used for access control in §6.3, see Discussion and related work).","locale":"en"},"4981":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-33","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Formal notion","locale":"en"},"4982":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-34","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Practical reading","locale":"en"},"4983":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-35","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"`Σ` as dependent partial map","locale":"en"},"4984":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-36","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"A service registry keyed by name, each key with a fixed value type.","locale":"en"},"4985":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-37","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"`set` precondition","locale":"en"},"4986":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-38","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Providing an already provided key is an error (\"single source\").","locale":"en"},"4987":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-39","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"`set` is an effect","locale":"en"},"4988":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-40","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"`provide(name, value)` returns a disposer and is tracked by the enclosing scope.","locale":"en"},"4989":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-41","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Satisfaction / notify","locale":"en"},"4990":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-42","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"A consumer is started only when every injected key is available; every provide/withdraw re-evaluates affected consumers.","locale":"en"},"4991":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-43","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Derived realization","locale":"en"},"4992":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-44","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"`ctx.isolate(...)` / `ctx.intercept(...)` return a *new child context object*; there is nothing to undo.","locale":"en"},"4993":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-45","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Realm","locale":"en"},"4994":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-46","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"An opaque token per (scope, key); the same token means a shared binding.","locale":"en"},"4995":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-47","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Interception merge","locale":"en"},"4996":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-48","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Declared metadata (in the consumer's `inject` declaration) merged with context metadata; outer → inner, inner wins.","locale":"en"},"4997":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects-49","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects#engineering-take-aways","content":"**In kardia:** a key is the string `Service::NAME` bound to a Rust type through the `Service` trait. `ctx.provide(value)` is a tracked effect returning an `EffectHandle`, and providing a key twice in one realm fails with `KardiaError::AlreadyProvided`. `ctx.isolate(key)` (a fresh private realm), `ctx.isolate_in(key, realm)` (a shared realm) and `ctx.intercept(key, meta)` return a derived `Context`. Declared metadata comes from `Inject::with_meta`, and `Service::merge_intercept` merges it with the context's, outermost first, by default a right-biased shallow merge of JSON objects. See Services.","locale":"en"},"4998":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","type":"page","content":"Discussion and related work","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","locale":"en"},"4999":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-0","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","content":"Paper §6–8: the system boundary, service multiplexing, access control, language requirements, dependency cycles and versioning, co-design with languages and operating systems, related work and the conclusion.","locale":"en"},"5000":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-1","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#system-boundary-61","content":"System boundary (§6.1)","locale":"en"},"5001":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-2","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#service-multiplexing-62","content":"Service multiplexing (§6.2)","locale":"en"},"5002":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-3","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#access-control-and-sandboxing-63","content":"Access control and sandboxing (§6.3)","locale":"en"},"5003":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-4","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#language-independence-and-selection-64-rust","content":"Language independence and selection (§6.4) [Rust]","locale":"en"},"5004":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-5","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#mutual-dependencies-and-component-granularity-65","content":"Mutual dependencies and component granularity (§6.5)","locale":"en"},"5005":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-6","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#dependency-typing-and-versioning-66","content":"Dependency typing and versioning (§6.6)","locale":"en"},"5006":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-7","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#co-design-with-languages-and-operating-systems-67","content":"Co-design with languages and operating systems (§6.7)","locale":"en"},"5007":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-8","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Related work (§7)","locale":"en"},"5008":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-9","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#conclusion-8","content":"Conclusion (§8)","locale":"en"},"5009":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-10","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","content":"This page summarises §6–8 of the paper (arXiv:2608.25512): theory and design guidance. The paper's notes that bear on a Rust port are marked **\\[Rust]** in the source summary; here each is followed by an \"In kardia\" callout saying how kardia realises it.","locale":"en"},"5010":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-11","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#system-boundary-61","content":"The boundary divides the environment into locations **inside** (the system can modify them exclusively *and* restore them, so they are tracked and revertible) and **outside** (either ability fails, so the operation acts as `id_Γ` and is untracked).","locale":"en"},"5011":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-12","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#system-boundary-61","content":"**Coeffects move the boundary** by reifying an external location behind operations that each have an inverse. The boundary is drawn *per location*, not per medium (a private scratch file is inside; a shared path is outside).","locale":"en"},"5012":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-13","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#system-boundary-61","content":"**Acquisition vs. emission.** Reaching outside happens in two stages: *acquisition* installs a record inside the boundary (`open` → descriptor, `malloc` → block, `fork` → child), which is revertible; *emission* pushes data through that channel (`write`, `send`) and crosses outside, which is not revertible.","locale":"en"},"5013":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-14","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#system-boundary-61","content":"**Withholding and compensation.** Recovering an emission requires either withholding it until the producing state is certain (the output-commit problem) or compensation (delete the created file, refund the charge) up to an application-supplied, coarser equivalence. Compensations compose LIFO like inverses, but the commutation metatheory (Metatheory) must be re-established against the coarser relation.","locale":"en"},"5014":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-15","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#service-multiplexing-62","content":"Coeffects echo OSGi services: providers publish under an interface, and consumers bind. Multiple implementations can be realised as:","locale":"en"},"5015":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-16","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#service-multiplexing-62","content":"**Exclusive binding**: at most one is bound at a time; switching unloads one provider and loads another, perturbing every consumer.","locale":"en"},"5016":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-17","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#service-multiplexing-62","content":"**Service broker**: a central service is injected by both the backing providers and the consumers; providers *register with the broker* through revertible effects; consumers see no change when a backing provider changes.","locale":"en"},"5017":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-18","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#service-multiplexing-62","content":"The broker enables **load balancing** (policy-driven dispatch; scale by loading and unloading providers), **rolling updates** (load the new provider, shift traffic, unload the old one once drained: quiescence/tranquility), and **cross-process invocation** (each process hosts its own context; RPC preserves the interface; it must be designed against an *asynchronous* contract).","locale":"en"},"5018":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-19","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#service-multiplexing-62","content":"**\\[Rust]** The broker pattern is the recommended way to get many providers per key within the single-provider calculus.","locale":"en"},"5019":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-20","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#service-multiplexing-62","content":"**In kardia:** one key has one provider per realm (a second `ctx.provide` fails with `KardiaError::AlreadyProvided`). A broker is an ordinary service whose registry is an `IdTable`; a backing provider registers with it through an effect created on its own fiber (`svc.caller().effect(..)`), so the registration is withdrawn when that provider unloads. kardia has no separate broker service for guests: each guest fiber's `GuestService` is its own RPC endpoint, and its calls are synchronous rather than the asynchronous contract above, so a hung guest blocks its caller. See Services and Guest plugins.","locale":"en"},"5020":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-21","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#access-control-and-sandboxing-63","content":"**Capability-based access control.** Proxy-mediated access only allows declared dependencies (an undeclared access is an error). `inject` acts as a capability request, the context proxy as a capability mediator. Because declarations are static, the orchestrator can review them at load time.","locale":"en"},"5021":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-22","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#access-control-and-sandboxing-63","content":"**Fine-grained policy through interception**: for example, a filesystem dependency carrying metadata about readable and writable paths, checked by the provider. It is adjustable from the context without modifying the provider; it affects how a dependency is used, not whether it is satisfied, so it can change at runtime without a reload.","locale":"en"},"5022":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-23","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#access-control-and-sandboxing-63","content":"**Sandboxing** untrusted code needs an execution boundary beyond the language (SFI, a separate runtime, a sandboxed process, a container); the untrusted component reaches host dependencies through a *bridge*, itself an ordinary fiber whose capabilities can be attenuated.","locale":"en"},"5023":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-24","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#language-independence-and-selection-64-rust","content":"**Temporal requirements:** closures (an inverse is a captured value), plus a way to introduce and retract a component's code at runtime:","locale":"en"},"5024":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-25","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#language-independence-and-selection-64-rust","content":"managed runtimes: a programmatic module registry (Node `require.cache`; ESM has no public eviction API);","locale":"en"},"5025":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-26","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#language-independence-and-selection-64-rust","content":"**native code: explicit dynamic linking and unlinking** (`dlopen`/`dlclose`, `LoadLibrary`/`FreeLibrary`);","locale":"en"},"5026":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-27","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#language-independence-and-selection-64-rust","content":"**WebAssembly**: an instance is released when a native embedder (for example **Wasmtime**) drops it.","locale":"en"},"5027":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-28","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#language-independence-and-selection-64-rust","content":"In all cases loading is an effect whose inverses undo the registrations the module introduced.","locale":"en"},"5028":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-29","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#language-independence-and-selection-64-rust","content":"**In kardia:** `kardia-hmr` loads guest modules through three backends, each running a guest plugin through a bridge plugin that instantiates the guest once per fiber and drops the instance when the fiber is disposed. `WasmSource` runs core WebAssembly modules on Wasmtime (sandboxed, one store per fiber). `DylibSource` loads dynamic libraries but never unloads them (no `dlclose`): unloading Rust code is unsound once it has registered thread-local destructors or handed out `'static` references, so each version is loaded from a fresh copy of the file. `ProcessSource` runs one child process per fiber and kills it after `dispose`. See Guest plugins and Hot reload.","locale":"en"},"5029":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-30","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#language-independence-and-selection-64-rust","content":"**Spatial requirements:** a dependency-injection mechanism at two levels:","locale":"en"},"5030":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-31","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#language-independence-and-selection-64-rust","content":"**type level**: a well-typed dependency access; the context type must record each key's coeffect. *Typeclasses (Haskell) and **traits (Rust)** let a provider extend the context type from its own module through an instance/`impl`*; TypeScript uses module augmentation.","locale":"en"},"5031":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-32","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#language-independence-and-selection-64-rust","content":"**runtime level**: access must be dynamically mediated (a JavaScript `Proxy`, Python descriptors, or reflection at the cost of type safety).","locale":"en"},"5032":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-33","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#language-independence-and-selection-64-rust","content":"**Metaprogramming** supplies both together: *compile-time metaprogramming (for example **Rust procedural macros**, Scala macros, Zig comptime) emits, for each dependency, a typed declaration together with an accessor, dispensing with a general-purpose interception primitive.*","locale":"en"},"5033":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-34","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#language-independence-and-selection-64-rust","content":"**In kardia:** the `Service` trait binds a type to its key (`#[derive(Service)]`), and a service crate extends what a consumer can do with an extension trait on `Svc<S>` (for example `TimerExt` for `Svc<Timer>`), which coherence allows because the trait is local. Access is mediated at runtime by `ctx.get::<S>()` against the committed view, without a proxy. The `#[plugin]` macro supplies the compile-time half: a plugin function taking `Injected<(A, B)>` declares `A` and `B`, and accessing anything else through it does not compile. See Plugins, Services and API design.","locale":"en"},"5034":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-35","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#mutual-dependencies-and-component-granularity-65","content":"A dependency cycle leaves the involved components **permanently inactive**. Unlike a concurrency deadlock it is *predictable from the declarations*, so a runtime can report it at load time. Most cycles decompose: for example, server ↔ access-controller becomes `server-core`, `access-control-core`, `request-mediation` (depending on both cores) and `policy-management` (depending on both). The cost is a potentially quadratic number of integration components, mitigated by package bundling, convention-based wiring and scaffolding.","locale":"en"},"5035":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-36","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#mutual-dependencies-and-component-granularity-65","content":"**In kardia:** the registry reports a cycle when a fiber is inserted: it searches the declared `provide`/`inject` of live fibers and logs a warning naming the cycle; the fiber stays `Pending`. See Lifecycle.","locale":"en"},"5036":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-37","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#dependency-typing-and-versioning-66","content":"Linking is purely **nominal** (by key). There are two problems across independently built components:","locale":"en"},"5037":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-38","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#dependency-typing-and-versioning-66","content":"**interface drift**: the provider changes the interface behind `k` between versions;","locale":"en"},"5038":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-39","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#dependency-typing-and-versioning-66","content":"**key collision**: unrelated providers choose the same key name.","locale":"en"},"5039":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-40","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#dependency-typing-and-versioning-66","content":"Approaches:","locale":"en"},"5040":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-41","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#dependency-typing-and-versioning-66","content":"**key namespacing** `K × P` (by package; eliminates collisions, couples to a registry);","locale":"en"},"5041":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-42","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#dependency-typing-and-versioning-66","content":"**peer dependencies** with semver ranges through the package manager (Cordis's current approach; relies on semver discipline and one version per package);","locale":"en"},"5042":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-43","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#dependency-typing-and-versioning-66","content":"**structural compatibility** predicates (width subtyping is easy; behavioural contracts are hard; bounded quantification is undecidable).","locale":"en"},"5043":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-44","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#dependency-typing-and-versioning-66","content":"This is an open problem.","locale":"en"},"5044":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-45","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#dependency-typing-and-versioning-66","content":"**\\[Rust]** Rust's `TypeId`/type-keyed services give collision-freedom *within one binary* (a form of key namespacing), but across dynamically loaded modules `TypeId` is not stable, so keys must then be strings plus an ABI/version tag.","locale":"en"},"5045":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-46","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#dependency-typing-and-versioning-66","content":"**In kardia:** keys are strings (`Service::NAME`) everywhere, because configuration names services. A binding holds its value as `dyn Any`, and a typed access downcasts it, failing with `AccessError::TypeMismatch`, which detects collisions between unrelated types within one binary. Guest modules do not share Rust types with the host: they provide and read services by string key, as JSON views with JSON calls, through the JSON guest ABI. That ABI carries no version tag. See Services and Guest plugins.","locale":"en"},"5046":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-47","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#co-design-with-languages-and-operating-systems-67","content":"**Languages:** make the context implicit again (for ergonomics, and to prevent a component from reaching another component's context through a closure or a global, which would leak effects out of its lifecycle); give the compiler syntax for effects (compile an effect iterator into one state machine holding the inverses in its frame instead of a closure per step); admit the coeffect specification into the type system (report cycles at compile time; structural dependency comparison à la row types).","locale":"en"},"5047":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-48","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#co-design-with-languages-and-operating-systems-67","content":"**Operating systems:** make the declared coeffect specification the whole of what a component can reach (like WASM imports), provide isolation and interception natively, and provide OS resources (memory, file descriptors) as coeffects so that acquisitions are recorded once by the party that hands them out; transactional or copy-on-write storage can make some emissions revertible.","locale":"en"},"5048":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-49","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Area","locale":"en"},"5049":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-50","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Representative work","locale":"en"},"5050":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-51","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Distinction from Cordis","locale":"en"},"5051":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-52","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Monadic effect systems","locale":"en"},"5052":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-53","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"ZIO, Effect-TS, fp-ts","locale":"en"},"5053":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-54","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Require writing inside the effect type; a withdrawn service leaves its effects in place. Cordis is an overlay on ordinary code and pairs every effect with an inverse.","locale":"en"},"5054":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-55","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Algebraic effects as capabilities","locale":"en"},"5055":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-56","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Effekt","locale":"en"},"5056":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-57","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Purpose (interpretation vs. tracking and reversion) and setting (static vs. runtime).","locale":"en"},"5057":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-58","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Reversible effect semantics","locale":"en"},"5058":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-59","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Heunen et al. (dagger/inverse arrows)","locale":"en"},"5059":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-60","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Global two-sided reversibility vs. a per-atomic-effect one-sided inverse supplied at application.","locale":"en"},"5060":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-61","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Graded types","locale":"en"},"5061":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-62","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Granule, coeffects for Java-like languages","locale":"en"},"5062":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-63","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Static annotations over fixed scopes.","locale":"en"},"5063":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-64","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Context-oriented programming","locale":"en"},"5064":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-65","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"COP layers","locale":"en"},"5065":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-66","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Changes method dispatch in dynamic extents; no tracking or reversion; activation is not dependency-driven. Cordis can express only COP's global, value-driven fragment.","locale":"en"},"5066":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-67","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Aspect-oriented programming","locale":"en"},"5067":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-68","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"AspectJ, dynamic AOP","locale":"en"},"5068":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-69","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Oblivious quantification vs. declared coeffects; Cordis's crosscutting is bound to a component's lifecycle.","locale":"en"},"5069":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-70","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Stateful forward migration","locale":"en"},"5070":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-71","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Quiescence/tranquility, DSU (Ginseng, Kitsune), Erlang `code_change`, Webpack/Vite HMR","locale":"en"},"5071":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-72","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Migrate state forward through hand-written functions; Cordis reverts and reapplies from a clean slate (in-memory state survives only in longer-lived dependencies; DSU-style migration on top of revertible effects is future work), but needs no migration code and supports full unloading.","locale":"en"},"5072":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-73","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Developer-authored recovery","locale":"en"},"5073":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-74","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"OSGi/IDE unload callbacks, Command pattern, sagas, effect-handler finalizers, event sourcing, React `useEffect`","locale":"en"},"5074":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-75","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"The inverse is an unenforced duty decoupled from the operation; `useEffect` pairs them but is not composable (top level only, no async or iterator).","locale":"en"},"5075":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-76","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Statically scoped reversal","locale":"en"},"5076":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-77","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"STM, reversible computing (Janus), RCCS, linear types, **RAII, Rust ownership**","locale":"en"},"5077":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-78","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Scope and reach are fixed statically. Cordis treats lexical resource management as *complementary*, appropriate for local resources within a component.","locale":"en"},"5078":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-79","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Interposed reclamation","locale":"en"},"5079":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-80","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Nooks, shadow drivers, Akeso","locale":"en"},"5080":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-81","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"The platform fixes what can be recorded; Cordis lets components introduce their own effects with inverses and propagates removal to dependents.","locale":"en"},"5081":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-82","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Initialization-time DI","locale":"en"},"5082":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-83","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Spring, Guice, Angular, Inversify, Vue provide/inject, React Context","locale":"en"},"5083":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-84","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"No reactive re-resolution, no lifecycle management.","locale":"en"},"5084":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-85","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Availability-reactive components","locale":"en"},"5085":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-86","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"OSGi Declarative Services, iPOJO, R-OSGi","locale":"en"},"5086":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-87","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"The closest precedent, but with hand-written, synchronous deactivation callbacks. Cordis reverts automatically and runs asynchronous teardown to completion (inertial Unloading).","locale":"en"},"5087":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-88","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Value-level reactivity","locale":"en"},"5088":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-89","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"FRP, signals","locale":"en"},"5089":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-90","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"Value granularity and glitch freedom per turn; Cordis is component-granular with an asynchronous lifecycle. Complementary (a coeffect may carry signals).","locale":"en"},"5090":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-91","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"**\\[Rust] note on RAII:** the paper explicitly positions Rust's ownership/RAII as *statically scoped* reversal: complementary, not a substitute. A Rust port should therefore *not* try to make a component's lifetime a lexical scope; component lifetime is dynamic and governed by the fiber state machine, while `Drop` remains useful for resources local to one effect.","locale":"en"},"5091":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-92","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#related-work-7","content":"**In kardia:** an `EffectHandle` does not dispose its effect on `Drop`; an effect lives until its fiber unloads or it is disposed explicitly, so a component's lifetime is never tied to a Rust scope. Inside an effect, ordinary `Drop` types still clean up local resources. See Effects and Lifecycle.","locale":"en"},"5092":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work-93","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-related-work#conclusion-8","content":"Revertible effects give local temporal composability (Revertible effects); reactive coeffects give local spatial composability (Reactive coeffects); the context paradigm and observational equivalence give independence (The context paradigm); the calculus (A calculus of dynamic composition) lifts both to whole systems with preservation, recovery exactness, ordering, coherence, progress and confluence (Metatheory). Cordis realises this (Implementation in the paper); Koishi validates it. The future validation target is **self-evolving agent harnesses**, which stress complete recovery under rapid replacement and dependency coordination under frequent topological change.","locale":"en"},"5093":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","type":"page","content":"Revertible effects","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","locale":"en"},"5094":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-0","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","content":"Paper §3.1: effects paired with inverses, the effect context, effect functions and effect iterators, which give a single component local temporal composability.","locale":"en"},"5095":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-1","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#from-impure-functions-to-context-transformations-311","content":"From impure functions to context transformations (§3.1.1)","locale":"en"},"5096":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-2","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-1--twisted-composition","content":"Definition 1 – twisted composition","locale":"en"},"5097":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-3","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-2--effect-context","content":"Definition 2 – effect context","locale":"en"},"5098":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-4","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-3--track","content":"Definition 3 – track","locale":"en"},"5099":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-5","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-6--recover","content":"Definition 6 – recover","locale":"en"},"5100":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-6","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#effect-functions-312","content":"Effect functions (§3.1.2)","locale":"en"},"5101":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-7","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-8--effect-function-and-witnessed-effect-function","content":"Definition 8 – effect function and witnessed effect function","locale":"en"},"5102":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-8","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-9--effect-composition-","content":"Definition 9 – effect composition ⋄","locale":"en"},"5103":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-9","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-12--effect-lifting-to-the-effect-context","content":"Definition 12 – effect (lifting to the effect context)","locale":"en"},"5104":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-10","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#effect-iterators-313","content":"Effect iterators (§3.1.3)","locale":"en"},"5105":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-11","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-17--effect-iterator","content":"Definition 17 – effect iterator","locale":"en"},"5106":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-12","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-18--effect-iterator-transformation","content":"Definition 18 – effect iterator transformation","locale":"en"},"5107":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-13","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#what-local-temporal-composability-means","content":"What local temporal composability means","locale":"en"},"5108":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-14","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Engineering take-aways","locale":"en"},"5109":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-15","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","content":"This page summarises §3.1 of the paper (arXiv:2608.25512). Definition and theorem numbers are the paper's. For how Cordis implements effects, see Effects in the Cordis reference; for kardia's Rust design, see Effects in the guide and API design.","locale":"en"},"5110":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-16","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","content":"Temporal composability requires that every modification a component makes be **trackable** and **recoverable**. The paper models an effect as a function `Γ → Γ × (Γ → Γ)`: given the current context it returns the modified context *and* an explicit inverse. Returning the inverse to the runtime makes the effect trackable; supplying it makes the effect revertible.","locale":"en"},"5111":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-17","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#from-impure-functions-to-context-transformations-311","content":"Any impure `f : X ⇝ Y` is rewritten into a pure `f : Γ × X → Γ × Y` over a context type `Γ`. For a fixed input `x`, the side effect is the endomorphism `γ ↦ pr₁(f(γ, x)) : Γ → Γ`. Effects therefore live in the **monoid of transformations** `(Γ → Γ, ∘, id_Γ)`:","locale":"en"},"5112":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-18","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#from-impure-functions-to-context-transformations-311","content":"closure: sequential composition of effects is an effect;","locale":"en"},"5113":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-19","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#from-impure-functions-to-context-transformations-311","content":"associativity: bracketing does not matter;","locale":"en"},"5114":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-20","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#from-impure-functions-to-context-transformations-311","content":"identity: `id_Γ` is the unit.","locale":"en"},"5115":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-21","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#from-impure-functions-to-context-transformations-311","content":"An undo for `f` is a **left inverse** `g` (`g ∘ f = id`, never required of `f ∘ g`); the paper simply calls it *the inverse*.","locale":"en"},"5116":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-22","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-1--twisted-composition","content":"Forwards compose normally, inverses in the opposite order. This makes `(Γ→Γ) × (Γ→Γ)` a monoid **`𝔗Γ`** (the product of the transformation monoid with its opposite), with unit `(id, id)`.","locale":"en"},"5117":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-23","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-2--effect-context","content":"`∂Γ := Γ × (Γ → Γ)`, a pair `(γ, φ)`:","locale":"en"},"5118":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-24","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-2--effect-context","content":"`γ`: the current state;","locale":"en"},"5119":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-25","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-2--effect-context","content":"`φ`: the **accumulator**, the composite of inverses so far; it recovers the initial state.","locale":"en"},"5120":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-26","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-2--effect-context","content":"The initial effect context is `(γ₀, id)`. Iterating gives the tower `Γ, ∂Γ, ∂²Γ, …`.","locale":"en"},"5121":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-27","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-3--track","content":"**Theorem 4**: tracking leaves the forward behaviour untouched: `pr₁ ∘ track(f,g) = f ∘ pr₁`.","locale":"en"},"5122":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-28","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-3--track","content":"**Theorem 5**: `track` is a monoid homomorphism `𝔗Γ → (∂Γ → ∂Γ)`: tracking pairs one at a time equals tracking their twisted composite.","locale":"en"},"5123":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-29","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-6--recover","content":"Apply the accumulator, then reset it.","locale":"en"},"5124":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-30","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-6--recover","content":"**Theorem 7**: for `g(f(γ)) = γ`: `recover(track(f,g)(γ,φ)) = recover(γ,φ)`. A tracked step whose inverse reverts it does not move the recovery target. The **soundness invariant** of a state is `φ(γ) = γ₀`; every state reached from `(γ₀, id)` by such steps satisfies it, and `recover` carries it back to `(γ₀, id)`.","locale":"en"},"5125":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-31","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-6--recover","content":"By Theorem 5 the same holds for whole sequences (eq. 11).","locale":"en"},"5126":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-32","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#effect-functions-312","content":"`track`/`recover` have two limitations:","locale":"en"},"5127":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-33","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#effect-functions-312","content":"`track(f, g)` fixes `g` before the state is seen: a single uniform inverse must work at every state. Real effects only need an inverse *for the state where they were applied* (for example \"remove the entry I just added, with the id I just drew\").","locale":"en"},"5128":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-34","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#effect-functions-312","content":"`recover` is all-or-nothing: you cannot undo one effect and keep the others.","locale":"en"},"5129":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-35","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#effect-functions-312","content":"The fixes: return the inverse at the point of application (input side, `Γ → ∂Γ`), and make the lifted effect itself return an inverse (output side, `∂Γ → ∂²Γ`). Both have the same shape.","locale":"en"},"5130":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-36","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-8--effect-function-and-witnessed-effect-function","content":"`𝔈Γ := Γ → Γ × (Γ → Γ)`, with `e(γ) = (δ, g)`: new context `δ`, inverse `g`.","locale":"en"},"5131":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-37","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-8--effect-function-and-witnessed-effect-function","content":"`𝔈Γ*` additionally carries a **witness** `(δ, g) = e(γ) ⇒ g(δ) = γ`: the inverse reverts the effect *at the state where it was applied*. A uniform inverse with `g ∘ f = id` is a special case.","locale":"en"},"5132":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-38","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-9--effect-composition-","content":"Run `g` then `f`, and compose the inverses so that `f`'s inverse runs first.","locale":"en"},"5133":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-39","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-9--effect-composition-","content":"**Theorem 10**: `(𝔈Γ, ⋄)` is a monoid with unit `η(γ) = (γ, id)`; `(f,g) ↦ γ ↦ (f(γ), g)` is a homomorphism `𝔗Γ → 𝔈Γ`.","locale":"en"},"5134":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-40","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-9--effect-composition-","content":"**Theorem 11**: `𝔈Γ*` is a submonoid (witnessing survives composition), and uniform inverses land in it.","locale":"en"},"5135":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-41","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-12--effect-lifting-to-the-effect-context","content":"The returned inverse is itself a *track* of the swapped pair: undoing is an effect (transform by `g`), and undoing the undo is performing the effect again (`pr₁ ∘ e`).","locale":"en"},"5136":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-42","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-12--effect-lifting-to-the-effect-context","content":"**Theorem 13**: `effect(f) ⋄ effect(g) = effect(f ⋄ g)`.","locale":"en"},"5137":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-43","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-12--effect-lifting-to-the-effect-context","content":"**Theorem 14**: projections commute: the lifted forward and inverse maps act on the state exactly as `f` and `g`.","locale":"en"},"5138":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-44","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-12--effect-lifting-to-the-effect-context","content":"**Theorem 15**: for a witnessed `e`: `g′(Δ) = (γ, φ ∘ g ∘ f)`. The *state* is recovered exactly; the *accumulator* is restored if and only if `g ∘ f = id`; in every case `(φ ∘ g ∘ f)(γ) = φ(γ)`, so the soundness invariant is preserved. Hence `effect` does **not** carry `𝔈Γ*` into `𝔈*∂Γ` in general, but reverting never moves the recovery target.","locale":"en"},"5139":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-45","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#effect-iterators-313","content":"A component's loading is not one effect but a **sequence**.","locale":"en"},"5140":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-46","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#effect-iterators-313","content":"**Theorem 16**: applying `e₁…eₙ ∈ 𝔈Γ*` from `(γ₀, id)` and reverting in **reverse order**, every revert recovers the state its application ran against, and every intermediate state satisfies the soundness invariant. LIFO needs nothing more.","locale":"en"},"5141":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-47","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-17--effect-iterator","content":"Each iteration yields `(δ, g, o)`: the new context, the inverse, and the continuation (`Nothing` = done, `Just(i)` = next).","locale":"en"},"5142":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-48","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-18--effect-iterator-transformation","content":"The accumulator becomes `φ ∘ g₁ ∘ … ∘ g_k`, which reverts in LIFO order (Theorem 16).","locale":"en"},"5143":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-49","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-18--effect-iterator-transformation","content":"`effect^iter` lands in the same `∂Γ → ∂²Γ` as `effect`, so **an iterator is an effect** and composes anywhere an effect can (nesting).","locale":"en"},"5144":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-50","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-18--effect-iterator-transformation","content":"The `Maybe(ℑ)` continuation makes a **boundary** available between any two iterations: at a boundary the accumulator recovers exactly the iterations so far. This is the hook used later for *interruption* (L-Divert, see the calculus) and *cancellation*.","locale":"en"},"5145":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-51","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-18--effect-iterator-transformation","content":"The iterator is a **reified delimited continuation**. Mainstream languages expose it through `yield`, so the model \"maps directly onto the generators they already provide\".","locale":"en"},"5146":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-52","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-18--effect-iterator-transformation","content":"A plain effect function embeds as an iterator whose first iteration yields `Nothing` (eq. 19).","locale":"en"},"5147":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-53","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#definition-18--effect-iterator-transformation","content":"**In kardia:** an effect body receives an `EffectScope`. `scope.defer(..)` collects an inverse without a boundary; `scope.yield_(disposer)` collects one and marks an iteration boundary, and `scope.checkpoint()` marks a boundary alone. Both return `Err(Interrupted)` once the effect has been disposed, so `?` ends the body at the boundary, keeping only the inverses collected so far. See Effects.","locale":"en"},"5148":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-54","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#what-local-temporal-composability-means","content":"> For every sequence of effect functions a component applies, the accumulator recovers the context it began at (Theorem 7), and reverting the sequence hands each inverse the state its own application ran against (Theorem 16). Loading a component is running one iterator and accumulating its inverses in `φ`; unloading it is applying `φ`.","locale":"en"},"5149":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-55","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#what-local-temporal-composability-means","content":"Two things are deliberately **left out** here and supplied later by *independence* (§3.4, see The context paradigm): reverting out of the order the accumulator imposes, and sequences that interleave the effects of other components.","locale":"en"},"5150":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-56","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Formal notion","locale":"en"},"5151":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-57","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Practical reading","locale":"en"},"5152":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-58","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Effect function returns inverse","locale":"en"},"5153":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-59","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Every registration API returns a disposer created *at the point of registration* (it can capture the id it just drew).","locale":"en"},"5154":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-60","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Witness","locale":"en"},"5155":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-61","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#engineering-take-aways","content":"An obligation on the author: the disposer must undo exactly that registration. Not checked at runtime.","locale":"en"},"5156":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-62","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#engineering-take-aways","content":"`⋄` / twisted composition","locale":"en"},"5157":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-63","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Disposers of a composite effect run in reverse order of their creation.","locale":"en"},"5158":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-64","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#engineering-take-aways","content":"`effect` returns an inverse at the next level","locale":"en"},"5159":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-65","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#engineering-take-aways","content":"`ctx.effect(...)` itself returns a disposer, and that disposer is registered in the *enclosing* scope's accumulator: effects nest.","locale":"en"},"5160":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-66","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Iterator boundary","locale":"en"},"5161":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-67","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#engineering-take-aways","content":"A multi-step setup can be cancelled *between* steps, keeping only the inverses of completed steps.","locale":"en"},"5162":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-68","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Soundness invariant","locale":"en"},"5163":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-69","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#engineering-take-aways","content":"Disposing twice is forbidden: an inverse applied at a state no application produced reverts nothing meaningful (the implementation enforces \"at most once\").","locale":"en"},"5164":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects-70","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts-revertible-effects#engineering-take-aways","content":"**In kardia:** `ctx.effect(label, |scope| async { .. })` and `ctx.effect_sync(..)` return an `EffectHandle` registered on the current fiber's accumulator; `scope.adopt(handle)` moves a nested effect under an enclosing one. A `Disposer` wraps an `FnOnce`, so \"at most once\" is enforced by the type system. See Effects and API design.","locale":"en"},"5165":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","type":"page","content":"Concepts","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Concepts"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts","locale":"en"},"5166":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-0","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts","content":"The theory behind kardia: the paper's problem statement, its contributions, how its sections map to the pages of this section, and its vocabulary.","locale":"en"},"5167":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-1","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts#problem-statement","content":"Problem statement","locale":"en"},"5168":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-2","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts#motivating-examples-12","content":"Motivating examples (§1.2)","locale":"en"},"5169":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-3","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts#core-idea","content":"Core idea","locale":"en"},"5170":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-4","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Pages in this section","locale":"en"},"5171":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-5","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts#preliminaries-2-in-brief","content":"Preliminaries (§2) in brief","locale":"en"},"5172":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-6","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Glossary","locale":"en"},"5173":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-7","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts","content":"kardia implements the model of the paper *A Programming Paradigm for Spatiotemporal Composability* (arXiv:2608.25512; 92 pages; Shi, Zhang and Cui; Peking University and DeepSeek-AI). This section summarises the paper's theory: its problem statement, contributions and vocabulary, then one page per part of the paper. Definition, theorem, lemma and algorithm numbers are always the paper's.","locale":"en"},"5174":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-8","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts","content":"This section does not describe the Cordis code base (see the Cordis reference) or kardia's Rust design (see Development). Where a concept has a direct counterpart in kardia, an \"In kardia\" callout says so and links the relevant page.","locale":"en"},"5175":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-9","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#problem-statement","content":"Static composition (function calls, imports, inheritance) is fixed at compile time. Plugin systems and *self-evolving agent harnesses* need **dynamic composition**: components are loaded, unloaded and reconfigured while the process keeps running. The paper argues that this has two orthogonal dimensions beyond the algebraic aspects of composition (§1.1):","locale":"en"},"5176":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-10","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#problem-statement","content":"Dimension","locale":"en"},"5177":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-11","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#problem-statement","content":"Requirement","locale":"en"},"5178":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-12","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#problem-statement","content":"Static analogue","locale":"en"},"5179":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-13","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#problem-statement","content":"**Temporal composability**","locale":"en"},"5180":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-14","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#problem-statement","content":"When a component is removed, every modification it made to the shared environment is completely and safely reversed (resources, event registrations, state mutations), in an orderly way.","locale":"en"},"5181":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-15","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#problem-statement","content":"Lexical scoping: RAII, bracket patterns","locale":"en"},"5182":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-16","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#problem-statement","content":"**Spatial composability**","locale":"en"},"5183":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-17","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#problem-statement","content":"Components declare, discover and resolve dependencies on one another in a structured, verifiable way; lifecycles are coordinated as dependencies appear, disappear or change identity.","locale":"en"},"5184":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-18","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#problem-statement","content":"Module import resolution","locale":"en"},"5185":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-19","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#motivating-examples-12","content":"**VSCode**: extensions cannot be unloaded individually (87 of the top 100 contain code and require an extension-host restart); `deactivate` is only a shutdown hook and separates disposal from creation. `extensionDependencies` is barely used (7 of the top 100), and inter-extension APIs are untyped (`exports: any`).","locale":"en"},"5186":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-20","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#motivating-examples-12","content":"**Agent harnesses**: a harness that rewrites its own components continuously cannot afford a restart per modification, and naive code replacement silently breaks dependents.","locale":"en"},"5187":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-21","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#motivating-examples-12","content":"**Coarse-grained workaround**: OS processes give temporal composability per process; container orchestrators give spatial composability per service. Both discard in-process state on restart and cannot express in-address-space dependencies; the granularity is wrong.","locale":"en"},"5188":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-22","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#core-idea","content":"Effect systems describe how a computation **modifies** its environment; coeffect systems describe what it **requires** from its environment. Both are classically *static* (checked over lexically fixed scopes). The paper **reifies** both as runtime objects:","locale":"en"},"5189":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-23","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#core-idea","content":"**Revertible effects** (§3.1): every context transformation is paired with an inverse that the runtime holds. Tracking and recovery are monoid homomorphisms, so the context is recovered upon removal, which gives *local temporal composability*.","locale":"en"},"5190":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-24","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#core-idea","content":"**Reactive coeffects** (§3.2): a component declares the keys it requires (a *specification*); every context change is classified against it as *activating*, *deactivating* or *neutral*, which drives activation and deactivation. This gives *local spatial composability*.","locale":"en"},"5191":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-25","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#core-idea","content":"**The context paradigm** (§3.3): the effect context and the coeffect context are unified into one context type `Γ∞`, and *every* effect and coeffect is mediated through it. This induces an **observational equivalence `≃`** under which the effects of distinct components become **independent** (§3.4).","locale":"en"},"5192":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-26","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#core-idea","content":"**A calculus of dynamic composition** (§4): a component is a triple `(d, p, e)` (dependencies, provisions, effect iterator); its instantiations are **fibers**; nine rules (3 orchestration, 6 lifecycle) move fibers through `Inactive → Reloading → Active → Unloading`. The metatheory proves preservation, recovery exactness, ordering, resolution coherence, progress (no deadlock and termination) and **confluence** (the quiescent state equals a from-scratch static load of the final configuration).","locale":"en"},"5193":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-27","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#core-idea","content":"**Cordis** (§5): a TypeScript meta-framework, made of a core library (effect tracking and coeffect resolution) and a declarative loader with configuration reconciliation and hot module replacement. Case study: **Koishi**, a chatbot framework with more than 4000 community plugins (Koishi uses Cordis v3; the paper describes v4).","locale":"en"},"5194":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-28","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Paper section","locale":"en"},"5195":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-29","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Topic","locale":"en"},"5196":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-30","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Page","locale":"en"},"5197":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-31","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"§1–2","locale":"en"},"5198":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-32","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Motivation, effects and coeffects preliminaries","locale":"en"},"5199":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-33","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"this page","locale":"en"},"5200":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-34","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"§3.1","locale":"en"},"5201":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-35","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Revertible effects (effect context, track/recover, effect functions, effect iterators)","locale":"en"},"5202":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-36","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Revertible effects","locale":"en"},"5203":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-37","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"§3.2","locale":"en"},"5204":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-38","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Reactive coeffects (coeffect context, specification and notification, isolation, interception)","locale":"en"},"5205":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-39","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Reactive coeffects","locale":"en"},"5206":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-40","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"§3.3–3.4","locale":"en"},"5207":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-41","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Unified context, coeffect operations, observational equivalence, independence, commutativity","locale":"en"},"5208":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-42","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"The context paradigm","locale":"en"},"5209":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-43","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"§4.1–4.2","locale":"en"},"5210":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-44","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Components, fibers, registry, the nine rules, guard, confinement","locale":"en"},"5211":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-45","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"A calculus of dynamic composition","locale":"en"},"5212":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-46","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"§4.3–4.4","locale":"en"},"5213":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-47","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Metatheory, extensions (asynchrony, failure, isolation, configuration)","locale":"en"},"5214":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-48","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Metatheory and extensions","locale":"en"},"5215":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-49","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"§5","locale":"en"},"5216":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-50","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Implementation as described by the paper (Algorithms 1–10), loader, HMR, Koishi","locale":"en"},"5217":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-51","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Implementation in the paper","locale":"en"},"5218":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-52","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"§6–8","locale":"en"},"5219":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-53","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Discussion, related work, conclusion","locale":"en"},"5220":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-54","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"Discussion and related work","locale":"en"},"5221":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-55","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"<Card title=\"Revertible effects\" href=\"/docs/concepts-revertible-effects\" description=\"Every effect returns its inverse; accumulated LIFO, the inverses recover the context a component started from.\" />","locale":"en"},"5222":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-56","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"<Card title=\"Reactive coeffects\" href=\"/docs/concepts-reactive-coeffects\" description=\"Components declare the keys they need and activate or deactivate as the context gains or loses them.\" />","locale":"en"},"5223":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-57","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"<Card title=\"The context paradigm\" href=\"/docs/concepts-context-paradigm\" description=\"One context mediates everything, so effects of different components are independent up to observational equivalence.\" />","locale":"en"},"5224":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-58","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"<Card title=\"A calculus of dynamic composition\" href=\"/docs/concepts-calculus\" description=\"Fibers, the registry and the nine rules, with the guard that makes providers outlive their consumers.\" />","locale":"en"},"5225":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-59","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"<Card title=\"Metatheory and extensions\" href=\"/docs/concepts-metatheory\" description=\"Preservation, recovery exactness, ordering, coherence, progress and confluence; asynchrony, failure, isolation, configuration.\" />","locale":"en"},"5226":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-60","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"<Card title=\"Implementation in the paper\" href=\"/docs/concepts-implementation\" description=\"Table 2 and Algorithms 1–10: how the paper says Cordis realises the calculus, the loader and HMR.\" />","locale":"en"},"5227":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-61","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#pages-in-this-section","content":"<Card title=\"Discussion and related work\" href=\"/docs/concepts-related-work\" description=\"System boundary, brokers, access control, language requirements, versioning, related work, with notes on Rust.\" />","locale":"en"},"5228":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-62","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#preliminaries-2-in-brief","content":"**Effects.** Judgments `Γ ⊢ t : T ! effect`. Monadic effects (Moggi, Wadler) encapsulate effectful computation in `T(A)` with unit `η` and multiplication `μ`. Algebraic effects (Plotkin and Power) declare operation signatures; effect handlers (Plotkin and Pretnar) interpret them with delimited continuations (Koka, Eff, OCaml 5).","locale":"en"},"5229":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-63","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#preliminaries-2-in-brief","content":"**Coeffects.** Judgments `Γ @ coeffect ⊢ t : T` annotate the *context*. Comonads `(D, ε, δ)` (Uustalu and Vene) structure context-dependent computation; Petricek et al. propose coeffects as a unified static analysis; graded coeffects use a pre-ordered semiring to quantify usage.","locale":"en"},"5230":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-64","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#preliminaries-2-in-brief","content":"**Gap.** Both are static instruments over lexically fixed scopes. No fixed lexical scope delimits a plugin loaded after deployment, and no compile-time context anticipates dependencies arising from runtime configuration. The paper therefore *reifies* the structures, so that a runtime can operate on them and establish dynamically what these systems guarantee statically.","locale":"en"},"5231":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-65","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"The paper's vocabulary:","locale":"en"},"5232":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-66","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Term","locale":"en"},"5233":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-67","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Meaning","locale":"en"},"5234":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-68","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Context type `Γ`","locale":"en"},"5235":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-69","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Runtime-operable reification of the typing context; `Γ∞ = μΓ. Γ × (Γ→Γ) × Σ`.","locale":"en"},"5236":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-70","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Effect context `∂Γ`","locale":"en"},"5237":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-71","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"`Γ × (Γ→Γ)`: a state plus an *accumulator* (composite of inverses).","locale":"en"},"5238":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-72","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Accumulator `φ`","locale":"en"},"5239":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-73","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Function that recovers the context to its initial state; LIFO composite of inverses.","locale":"en"},"5240":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-74","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Effect function `𝔈Γ`","locale":"en"},"5241":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-75","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"`Γ → Γ × (Γ→Γ)`: returns the new state and a per-state inverse. Witnessed (`𝔈Γ*`) when the inverse provably reverts at the applied state.","locale":"en"},"5242":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-76","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Effect iterator `ℑΓ`","locale":"en"},"5243":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-77","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"`μℑ. Γ → Γ × (Γ→Γ) × Maybe(ℑ)`: a sequence of effects with a boundary between consecutive iterations; a reified delimited continuation (generators).","locale":"en"},"5244":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-78","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Coeffect context `Σ`","locale":"en"},"5245":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-79","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Finite dependent partial map `(k:K) ⇀ V_k`.","locale":"en"},"5246":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-80","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Specification `d`","locale":"en"},"5247":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-81","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"`Set(K)`: keys a component requires.","locale":"en"},"5248":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-82","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en