, entry state machine, outer/inner fibers, catalog, managed realms + migration, write-back hooks, `loader` barrier; `kardia` binary bootstrapping a root with the loader","locale":"en"},"5798":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"loader specs (minus expressions) green","locale":"en"},"5799":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"**5. Include & fs**","locale":"en"},"5800":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"`kardia-fs` watcher; `kardia-include` parse/journal/patch/sync/atomic write/locations; `kardia.yml` bootstrap identical to `core/bin.js`","locale":"en"},"5801":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"include + fs specs green; editing `kardia.yml` live reconfigures a running app","locale":"en"},"5802":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"**6. Ergonomics**","locale":"en"},"5803":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"`kardia-macros`: `#[plugin]`/`#[service]` derive (NAME, inject tuples, extension traits), compile-time undeclared-access errors for typed deps; optional expression language","locale":"en"},"5804":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"macro tests (trybuild)","locale":"en"},"5805":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"**7. Dynamic modules (optional)**","locale":"en"},"5806":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"`ModuleSource`, `ModuleGraph`, ported HMR reconcile; WASM backend prototype; dylib dev backend","locale":"en"},"5807":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"`reconcile.spec` ported against an in-memory source","locale":"en"},"5808":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"Phases 1–3 deliver the paper's core library; 4–5 the paper's loader; 7 the paper's HMR.","locale":"en"},"5809":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#phases","content":"Names as they ended up: `try_get_live` is `live`, the root is created with `Context::new()` (there is no public `Root`), and the service derive is `#[derive(Service)]`. The example app of Phase 3 is `crates/kardia/examples/provider_consumer.rs` (`cargo run -p kardia --example provider_consumer`).","locale":"en"},"5810":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"All phases above are implemented in `crates/` (`cargo test --workspace`; `cargo run -p kardia -- examples/kardia.yml` boots an app from a watched config file).","locale":"en"},"5811":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"Every case of every TypeScript spec has been ported, found already covered, or judged not applicable. The not-applicable cases test JavaScript or Node mechanics with no Rust counterpart:","locale":"en"},"5812":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"decorators, prototypes and Proxy-based callable services, `this` binding;","locale":"en"},"5813":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"waterfall and the deprecated `dispatch` API;","locale":"en"},"5814":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"Node module resolution and loader internals (`resolve`, `import`, `create`, `package-manager`, `loader-utils`);","locale":"en"},"5815":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"JavaScript stack frames;","locale":"en"},"5816":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"concurrent reads of one async iterator.","locale":"en"},"5817":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"The current workspace contains 17 library/application crates plus the unpublished `xtask`. Published crate versions are listed under Releases; the newly added HTTP/Server crates are source additions and were not released in this work.","locale":"en"},"5818":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#status","content":"CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs `cargo fmt --check`, clippy with warnings as errors, `cargo xtask arch` and the test suite on pushes and pull requests that change Rust code, on a pinned toolchain (Rust 1.99.1), because the compile-error snapshots of `kardia-macros` change with the compiler. Pushing a `v*` tag runs `release.yml`: it reuses CI's run of the tagged commit, then runs `cargo publish --workspace` for every crate version crates.io does not have yet, waiting out its limit on new crates, and creates the GitHub release. The workflow can also be started by hand to resume a release that stopped part-way. Contributing describes the workflows step by step.","locale":"en"},"5819":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Where the implementation departs from this plan or from the TypeScript code. Three entries are long and have their own subsections below the table.","locale":"en"},"5820":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Topic","locale":"en"},"5821":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Implementation","locale":"en"},"5822":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Retired providers","locale":"en"},"5823":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Found by the property tests: retiring a fiber unbinds its live provisions at once, so a successor can provide in the same realm while old consumers still drain (they read through their committed views). The TS code keeps the slot until the drain and fails the successor.","locale":"en"},"5824":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Stale loads","locale":"en"},"5825":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"A retired fiber's load still in flight may register inverses (so nothing it acquired leaks), but not provisions.","locale":"en"},"5826":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Provision removal during a drain","locale":"en"},"5827":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Postponed to the end of the provider's drain, so descendants can reach inherited services during their own cleanup without relying on microtask order.","locale":"en"},"5828":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Re-pointable contexts","locale":"en"},"5829":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Scope nodes carry their fiber; the fiber chain of an access is read off the derivation chain, so moving an entry also moves the fibers below it (the TS prototype behaviour). Entry tables are mutable `Overlay` nodes.","locale":"en"},"5830":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Checked typed access","locale":"en"},"5831":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"`Injected<D>::get::<S, _>()` (the `_` is the inferred position of `S` in `D`); an undeclared `S` does not compile.","locale":"en"},"5832":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-61","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Expressions","locale":"en"},"5833":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-62","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"A side-effect-free JavaScript subset (`kardia_loader::expr`) over JSON views of services; no function calls.","locale":"en"},"5834":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-63","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Errors","locale":"en"},"5835":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-64","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Config-file errors and loader-logged entry errors carry `file:line:col` locations as text. See Errors.","locale":"en"},"5836":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-65","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Loader API","locale":"en"},"5837":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-66","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Resolving a parent group waits for a group entry whose plugin is still loading (its group exists only once its `apply` runs).","locale":"en"},"5838":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-67","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Service-to-service calls","locale":"en"},"5839":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-68","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"A service resolves its own dependencies through the context it stored (its definition site), so interception metadata of the outer caller does not reach the services it calls in turn (it stays on `Svc::caller()`), and effects a service creates through its stored context belong to the service's fiber. In TS, both follow the use site.","locale":"en"},"5840":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-69","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Events","locale":"en"},"5841":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-70","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"`emit` and `bail` log a panicking listener and continue, `serial` returns its panic as an error, `parallel` collects it (see Dispatching); TS lets a throwing listener abort the dispatch. There is no generic `waterfall`; typed hooks (`on_config`, `on_update`) replace it.","locale":"en"},"5842":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-71","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Logger","locale":"en"},"5843":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-72","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Messages are formatted by the caller (`impl Display`), so exporters receive finished text: no `%`-directives or custom formatters.","locale":"en"},"5844":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-73","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Loader hooks","locale":"en"},"5845":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-74","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"`loader/patch-context` is `Loader::on_patch_context(ctx, hook)`; `EntryHandle::init`; an hmr case without a Rust counterpart. See Loader hooks.","locale":"en"},"5846":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-75","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Model tests","locale":"en"},"5847":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-76","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"The property tests drive real tokio schedules with random delays rather than a deterministic scheduler. They check ordering during every run, and preservation, recovery exactness and confluence (the active fibers are exactly the support set) at quiescence.","locale":"en"},"5848":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-77","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"Dynamic modules","locale":"en"},"5849":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-78","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#deviations","content":"`ModuleSource` / `ModuleGraph` / `Hmr`, three file backends (WASM, dylib, process) sharing one JSON guest ABI, the `kardia-guest` SDK and watch mode. See Dynamic modules.","locale":"en"},"5850":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-79","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#errors","content":"Parse and validation errors of config files carry `file:line:col`.","locale":"en"},"5851":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-80","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#errors","content":"Errors the loader logs for an entry (option and config validation, `ModuleNotFound`, expression failures) are located through `TreeBackend::locate` / `locate_expr`:","locale":"en"},"5852":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-81","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#errors","content":"each issue gets `(at path, file:line:col)`;","locale":"en"},"5853":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-82","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#errors","content":"an expression failure gets an `at <path> (file:line:col)` frame;","locale":"en"},"5854":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-83","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#errors","content":"the entry and each ancestor that has a position get an `at <id> (file:line:col)` frame,","locale":"en"},"5855":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-84","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#errors","content":"as text instead of rewritten JS stacks.","locale":"en"},"5856":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-85","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#errors","content":"The include locates values where their owner writes them (its file, or a patch in its parent's file), aliases at their anchors, expression chars through YAML escapes. Values changed at runtime are not located. `Entry::evaluate` is not exposed.","locale":"en"},"5857":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-86","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#loader-hooks","content":"The `loader/patch-context` waterfall is `Loader::on_patch_context(ctx, hook)`: a hook gets the entry and the rest of the chain as a future, which builds the rest only when awaited; hooks run in registration order inside the loader's own isolation step and are removed with `ctx`'s fiber. Unlike TS, the new isolation table is installed together with the re-parenting at the end of the chain, not before the hooks run.","locale":"en"},"5858":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-87","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#loader-hooks","content":"`EntryHandle::init` starts an entry or joins its pending start.","locale":"en"},"5859":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-88","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#loader-hooks","content":"The hmr `index` case \"waits for a second re-enable init\" has no hmr decision to wait on in Rust (the transaction has decided once the held unload completes); its port guards that the re-enables leave one live instance and hmr adds no apply.","locale":"en"},"5860":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-89","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"`ModuleSource` / `ModuleGraph` / `Hmr`, plus three file backends that share one JSON guest ABI (documented in the `kardia-hmr` crate docs) and a bridge plugin instantiating the guest per fiber.","locale":"en"},"5861":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-90","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"Backend","locale":"en"},"5862":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-91","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"How it works","locale":"en"},"5863":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-92","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"`WasmSource`","locale":"en"},"5864":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-93","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"Runs core WASM modules on wasmtime, one store per fiber, instead of WIT components, so guests need no component toolchain (the tests are written in WAT).","locale":"en"},"5865":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-94","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"`DylibSource`","locale":"en"},"5866":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-95","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"Speaks a C ABI instead of passing Rust types: a cdylib carries its own copies of `kardia-core` and tokio, with separate statics and no runtime context. Each version is loaded from a unique copy and never unloaded.","locale":"en"},"5867":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-96","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"`ProcessSource`","locale":"en"},"5868":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-97","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"Runs executables, one child process per fiber, speaking the same ABI as JSON lines over stdin and stdout, in protocol version 1 or `kardia-guest/2` (request ids, cancellation, pings). Respawn = reload; a guest whose process ends fails its own fiber (`Fiber::fail`, with an exit report), and a guest that breaks the protocol, exceeds the frame limit or misses a call's deadline is killed. `ProcessGuest` adds arguments, a `Spawner`, timeouts and metadata given by the host. There is no separate broker service: each fiber's `GuestService` is the RPC endpoint.","locale":"en"},"5869":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-98","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"`GuestService::call` blocks the caller until the guest answers; `call_async` is closer to the asynchronous RPC contract of paper §6.2: it runs off the async workers, with a deadline and cancellation, and the bridge runs `apply` and `dispose` the same way. Process guests do their I/O on threads of their own, so a deadline holds whichever thread waits. The transport traits (`GuestCode`, `GuestInstance`) are public, for a host's own transport, and so is each backend's guest code (`ProcessGuest`, `WasmGuest`), so a host can give a guest's metadata itself. An instance that fails (a trap, fuel, time or memory used up, a process that ended or was killed) fails its call with a typed `GuestFailure`, apart from the guest's own errors; the threads kardia-hmr starts catch their panics, which become the failure of the guest they serve; a guest provides only the services its metadata declares; wasmtime's process-wide trap handling on macOS (Mach ports or signals) is one setting for every engine kardia-hmr makes (Guest plugins). The `kardia` binary chooses a backend by file extension and does not load process guests.","locale":"en"},"5870":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-99","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"Guest services are JSON views with JSON calls (`GuestService`). `kardia-guest` is the Rust guest SDK: a `Guest` trait and `export!`, which emits the WASM exports on wasm32 and the C symbols elsewhere, so one source builds for both backends; `serve` serves the process transport. Unlike the other crates (GPL-3.0-only), it is licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0.","locale":"en"},"5871":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-100","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"Watch mode (`HmrConfig { watch: { enabled, files, debounce } }`) watches the scoped `fs.baseUrl` directory and reloads each debounce window as one batch. HMR requires `std:fs`; include scopes it to the included file’s directory. Override the directory with an FS intercept. `HmrConfig.base` and the loader tree’s independent `base_dir` interface have been removed. Failed imports retry in the next batch; host-module changes are only reported.","locale":"en"},"5872":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-101","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"The `kardia` binary registers `hmr` and resolves any entry name that is not a built-in plugin as a guest module relative to the config file: `examples/kardia.yml` hot-reloads `plugins/hello.wat`.","locale":"en"},"5873":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-102","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#dynamic-modules","content":"For using guests, see Guest plugins and Hot reload.","locale":"en"},"5874":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-103","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`js/` holds the npm packages `@kardiajs/core`, `@kardiajs/timer`, `@kardiajs/bridge`, `@kardiajs/tauri` and `@kardiajs/vue`, and `crates/kardia-bridge` the Rust host of the bridge (TypeScript and Tauri). They are versioned and released apart from the crates (Contributing).","locale":"en"},"5875":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-104","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`@kardiajs/core` ports Kardia's core (its state machine, checked access, effect engine and logger), not the TypeScript Cordis code. It does not depend on Cordis, at run time or in development: this repository never did (`cordis/` is a local, gitignored copy for reference), and the packages have no runtime dependencies. Its test suite ports the relevant Cordis specs. Where it differs:","locale":"en"},"5876":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-105","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Topic","locale":"en"},"5877":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-106","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`@kardiajs/core`","locale":"en"},"5878":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-107","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Service access","locale":"en"},"5879":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-108","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`ctx.get(key)` / `ctx.live(key)` instead of property access through a `Proxy` context. A plugin defined with `definePlugin` gets a context typed by its `inject`, so an undeclared access is a type error (Kardia's `Injected<D>`).","locale":"en"},"5880":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-109","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Caller attribution","locale":"en"},"5881":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-110","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"A `Service` comes back from `ctx.get` as a view whose `caller` is the accessing context (Kardia's `Svc::caller`). Views are `Proxy` objects, so a service's `#private` fields do not pass through them; use `private`.","locale":"en"},"5882":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-111","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`apply`","locale":"en"},"5883":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-112","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"May return a promise, or a function that runs when the plugin stops (as in Cordis; Kardia's `apply` returns only its result). Classes are instantiated per fiber; there is no `Service.init`.","locale":"en"},"5884":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-113","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Effects","locale":"en"},"5885":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-114","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`ctx.effect(scope => ...)` with `scope.defer`, `scope.adopt` and `scope.checkpoint`, or a returned disposer (Kardia's `effect_sync` / `effect` in one). No generator or async-iterator effects.","locale":"en"},"5886":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-115","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Internal events","locale":"en"},"5887":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-116","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`internal/plugin-inserted` and `internal/plugin-removed` (Kardia's `PluginInserted` / `PluginRemoved`) instead of Cordis' single `internal/plugin`; `internal/status` passes the old and new state.","locale":"en"},"5888":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-117","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Events","locale":"en"},"5889":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-118","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"As in Kardia: `emit` logs a failing listener and runs the rest; no `waterfall`; update hooks are `ctx.onUpdate`. No decorators, no `this` binding of listeners.","locale":"en"},"5890":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-119","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Recovery","locale":"en"},"5891":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-120","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`fiber.setRecovery` supports manual (default), bounded retry and asynchronous host decisions; `fiber.fail` reports instance failure. Timer callback errors are operation failures, and disposal awaits in-flight callbacks. Available in source, not yet in a new npm release; see details.","locale":"en"},"5892":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-121","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`update` / `restart`","locale":"en"},"5893":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-122","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Throw at once for an invalid config or a retired fiber (Kardia's `Result`); the promise they return, which settles with the fiber, is marked handled, so dropping it never raises an unhandled rejection.","locale":"en"},"5894":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-123","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"`ctx.inject(deps, apply)`","locale":"en"},"5895":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-124","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Creates a plugin of its own on every call, so `registry.replace` cannot target it.","locale":"en"},"5896":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-125","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Names","locale":"en"},"5897":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-126","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"A fiber keeps its plugin's name after its last fiber is retired, so the events of its removal still name it.","locale":"en"},"5898":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-127","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Loader, include, HMR","locale":"en"},"5899":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-128","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"Not ported: they read files, and the client runs in a webview. Vite's HMR can drive `registry.replace`.","locale":"en"},"5900":{"id":"/en/docs/development-status-129","page_id":"/en/docs/development-status","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-status#typescript","content":"The bridge's calls are asynchronous, unlike the guest ABI's (Dynamic modules): it is the asynchronous contract of paper §6.2. Its end-to-end tests drive the real Rust host from the TypeScript client.","locale":"en"},"5901":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","type":"page","content":"Testing strategy","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-testing","locale":"en"},"5902":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing","content":"How kardia is tested: the ported TypeScript spec suite and its runtimes, model-based property tests of the paper's metatheory, unit tests and tooling.","locale":"en"},"5903":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Port the TypeScript suite (acceptance)","locale":"en"},"5904":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Model-based tests of the paper's metatheory","locale":"en"},"5905":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-testing#unit-tests","content":"Unit tests","locale":"en"},"5906":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-testing#tooling","content":"Tooling","locale":"en"},"5907":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-testing#server","content":"Server parity and failure tests","locale":"en"},"5908":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"JS recovery and cross-runtime isolation","locale":"en"},"5909":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing","content":"kardia's tests check the implementation against two specifications: the TypeScript test suite of Cordis (behaviour) and the paper's theorems (semantics). The tests exercise the real implementation; mocks are kept to a minimum.","locale":"en"},"5910":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Each phase ports the corresponding `*.spec.ts` files as Rust integration tests, keeping the snake_case of each TS test name for traceability. The runtime follows what a test exercises:","locale":"en"},"5911":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Runtime","locale":"en"},"5912":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Used for","locale":"en"},"5913":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"**`current_thread`** (`#[tokio::test]`)","locale":"en"},"5914":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"The default: core, loader and hmr tests. Interleavings stay close to the single JS thread.","locale":"en"},"5915":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"**Paused time** (`start_paused = true`)","locale":"en"},"5916":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Tests that depend on timers: the timer suite, and the core and loader tests whose effects wait on timers.","locale":"en"},"5917":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"**`multi_thread`**","locale":"en"},"5918":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Where real parallelism is the point: every include test, plus the fs and hmr watch cases that need it (e.g. a module load held open with `block_in_place`). The include's IO loop and the file watch then run alongside tree updates, as in the `kardia` binary. These tests found the races fixed in the include's write path and the loader's update path.","locale":"en"},"5919":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"**Real time**","locale":"en"},"5920":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"File-system watch tests, since `notify` reports events from its own thread. An expected event is awaited with bounded polling (`eventually` helpers); a fixed wait is used only to check that something does *not* happen.","locale":"en"},"5921":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"The specs per phase (the phases are on Status and deviations):","locale":"en"},"5922":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Phase","locale":"en"},"5923":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Specs to port","locale":"en"},"5924":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"1","locale":"en"},"5925":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"`dispose.spec` (effect shapes, abort at boundaries, finally/cleanup, errors)","locale":"en"},"5926":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"2","locale":"en"},"5927":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"`fiber.spec` (inertia locks 1–3, failure, update, dispose of pending/failed), `update.spec` (concurrent updates), `service.spec`, `service-dispose.spec` (guards, drain ordering, concurrent sibling cleanup), `plugin.spec`, `replace.spec`, `isolate.spec`, `reflect.spec`","locale":"en"},"5928":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"3","locale":"en"},"5929":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"`events.spec`, `intercept.spec`, `logger.spec`, `shadow.spec` (re-expressed as def-site/use-site tests on `Svc`), timer `index.spec`","locale":"en"},"5930":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"4","locale":"en"},"5931":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"loader `validate.spec`, `commit.spec`, `group.spec`, `index.spec`, `evaluate.spec`, `rebuild.spec`, `isolate*.spec`","locale":"en"},"5932":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"5","locale":"en"},"5933":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"include `sync.spec`, `patch.spec`, `reload.spec`, `write.spec` (fault injection on rename), `parse.spec`; fs `watch.spec`","locale":"en"},"5934":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"7","locale":"en"},"5935":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"hmr `reconcile.spec` (with an in-memory `ModuleSource`)","locale":"en"},"5936":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#port-the-suite","content":"Every case of every spec has been ported, found already covered, or judged not applicable; see Implementation status.","locale":"en"},"5937":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Because the lifecycle is written as pure critical sections (Concurrency), the core can be driven by a **deterministic scheduler** in tests: a harness that holds spawned transitions in a queue and lets `proptest` choose which runnable task advances next. With synthetic components whose effects are writes to a shared table (each with an exact inverse), random orchestration sequences (insert / retire / provide / withdraw / update) and random schedules check the theorems of the metatheory:","locale":"en"},"5938":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Property","locale":"en"},"5939":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Theorem","locale":"en"},"5940":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Checked as","locale":"en"},"5941":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"**Preservation**","locale":"en"},"5942":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Thm 64","locale":"en"},"5943":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Registry well-formedness after every step (parents exist, provisions disjoint per realm, committed views name installed fibers).","locale":"en"},"5944":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"**Recovery exactness**","locale":"en"},"5945":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Thm 68 / Cor 69","locale":"en"},"5946":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"After a fiber's episode closes, the table equals the run with that fiber's steps removed; a removed fiber's provisions are empty.","locale":"en"},"5947":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"**Ordering**","locale":"en"},"5948":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Thm 70","locale":"en"},"5949":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"A provider's episode strictly contains each consumer's episode that committed to it; no consumer reads a binding outside its provider's episode.","locale":"en"},"5950":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"**Resolution coherence**","locale":"en"},"5951":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Thm 71","locale":"en"},"5952":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Every `apply` observes one committed view.","locale":"en"},"5953":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"**Progress**","locale":"en"},"5954":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Thm 73","locale":"en"},"5955":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Every schedule reaches quiescence within the step bound (acyclic declarations).","locale":"en"},"5956":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"**Confluence**","locale":"en"},"5957":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"Thm 80","locale":"en"},"5958":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"For fixed orchestration inputs, all schedules reach the same set of `Active` fibers (= the support set A) and the same table up to fiber renaming.","locale":"en"},"5959":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"These tests check the implementation against the theory with no business logic copied into the tests.","locale":"en"},"5960":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#model-based","content":"As implemented (`crates/kardia-core/tests/model.rs`), the property tests drive real tokio schedules with random delays rather than a deterministic scheduler. They check ordering during every run, and preservation, recovery exactness and confluence (the active fibers are exactly the support set) at quiescence. The property tests also found the retired-provider deviation.","locale":"en"},"5961":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#unit-tests","content":"Pure modules (`IdTable`, `Scope` lookups, target computation, `journal`, `patch`, glob predicate, HMR union-find) get focused unit tests. Mocks are limited to `Catalog`/`ModuleSource` test registries and fault-injecting filesystem shims.","locale":"en"},"5962":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#tooling","content":"`cargo fmt`, `cargo clippy -D warnings`, `cargo test --workspace` (the exact CI commands are on Contributing).","locale":"en"},"5963":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-61","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#tooling","content":"`unsafe` is confined to the FFI of the dylib backend (`kardia-hmr/src/dylib.rs`) and the guest SDK (`kardia-guest/src/ffi.rs`); every other crate forbids it.","locale":"en"},"5964":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-62","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#tooling","content":"Optional `loom` is *not* planned: the single-lock design has no lock-free state to model.","locale":"en"},"5965":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-63","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#server","content":"The five Server packages have 105 original upstream declarations and 201 real supplement/dual-host tests on Linux, Windows and macOS. This includes full MIME fixtures, TLS, WS, CLI integration and all three recovery policies while unrelated plugins continue working. See commands and evidence; regenerate inventory/MIME sources with the existing scripts, never by hand.","locale":"en"},"5966":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-64","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"`js.yml` runs package type checks, tests, builds and real Rust subprocess bridge E2E on Linux, Windows and macOS. It verifies recovery in each runtime independently of the manual upstream Server comparison workflow.","locale":"en"},"5967":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-65","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"Local acceptance passed 155 tests in 14 JS files, 10 real cross-runtime E2E tests, and 11 Rust bridge tests plus clippy, fmt and architecture checks. New coverage includes:","locale":"en"},"5968":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-66","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"17 core tests: repeated failure under all three policies, recovery after cleanup, independent plugin progress, default manual handling, failed loads, no unchanged validation retries, throwing/rejecting host handlers, and cancellation on policy changes or removal.","locale":"en"},"5969":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-67","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"15 timer tests: synchronous/asynchronous errors across all four schedulers, caller attribution and awaiting callbacks on disposal; repeated explicit instance failures under all three policies.","locale":"en"},"5970":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-68","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"5 additional bridge tests: three manual/retry/host recovery rounds in a real Rust provider, JS consumer withdrawal/recreation, uninterrupted independent Rust/JS calls and timer progress without restarting the bridge, plus removal cancelling retry/host recovery.","locale":"en"},"5971":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-69","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"Some existing unit tests use fake timers; the new fault tests use real timers and implementations, and E2E uses real subprocess communication. Native CI does not constitute independent testing on the user's devices, in a browser or in a Tauri webview. See policies and same-thread boundaries.","locale":"en"},"5972":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-70","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"Native acceptance: all six jobs in JS #43707771734 passed at `3b67c40` (155 package tests, five package builds and 10 E2E tests on each platform). All three jobs in Rust CI #43707335399 passed at `fd1a6e3`; subsequent changes only touched JS supervision, tests and documentation, leaving Rust source unchanged. Site #43707771760 passed type checks, 41 tests, the full build and publishing. No versions were bumped or npm/crates packages published in this work.","locale":"en"},"5973":{"id":"/en/docs/development-testing-71","page_id":"/en/docs/development-testing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-testing#js-recovery","content":"Native validation found Windows LICENSE copying failed because the existing build script concatenated path separators; native paths and a portable compiler invocation fixed it. The E2E harness now drains shutdown requests to avoid Windows EPIPE, and observes connection events after initial readiness to exclude macOS startup retries. Assertions for no reconnection during failures, independent progress and cleanup remain intact. Cancelled intermediate runs are not acceptance evidence.","locale":"en"},"5974":{"id":"/en/docs/development","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"page","content":"Rust design","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development","locale":"en"},"5975":{"id":"/en/docs/development-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"The design of kardia, the Rust implementation of Cordis: goals, the concept mapping from the paper to Rust, and the crate layout.","locale":"en"},"5976":{"id":"/en/docs/development-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development#goals","content":"Goals, non-goals, guiding decisions","locale":"en"},"5977":{"id":"/en/docs/development-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development#goals-list","content":"Goals","locale":"en"},"5978":{"id":"/en/docs/development-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development#non-goals","content":"Non-goals (for the first releases)","locale":"en"},"5979":{"id":"/en/docs/development-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"Guiding decisions","locale":"en"},"5980":{"id":"/en/docs/development-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development#concept-mapping","content":"Concept mapping: paper → Cordis (TS) → Rust","locale":"en"},"5981":{"id":"/en/docs/development-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development#layout","content":"Workspace layout","locale":"en"},"5982":{"id":"/en/docs/development-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development#dependencies","content":"Dependency direction","locale":"en"},"5983":{"id":"/en/docs/development-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development#external-dependencies","content":"External dependencies","locale":"en"},"5984":{"id":"/en/docs/development-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"This section is the design of kardia, the Rust re-implementation of the Cordis framework. It builds on","locale":"en"},"5985":{"id":"/en/docs/development-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"the **theory**: the Concepts section (the paper's definitions, rules and theorems), and","locale":"en"},"5986":{"id":"/en/docs/development-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"the **reference implementation**: the Cordis reference section (TypeScript, Cordis v4),","locale":"en"},"5987":{"id":"/en/docs/development-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"and is explicit about which of the two it follows where they differ (see Paper vs. code). The design is implemented in `crates/`; Status and deviations records the status and where the implementation departs from this text.","locale":"en"},"5988":{"id":"/en/docs/development-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"The section started as a plan written before the code. Its text is kept as the record of the design. Where the code in `crates/` now differs from a signature or sketch, a note below the sketch says so, and Status and deviations lists the deliberate departures.","locale":"en"},"5989":{"id":"/en/docs/development-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"Concurrency\" href=\"/docs/development-concurrency\" description=\"One lock per root, critical sections that return actions, lock ordering.\" />","locale":"en"},"5990":{"id":"/en/docs/development-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"Core data structures\" href=\"/docs/development-lifecycle\" description=\"Identifiers, scopes, bindings, fibers, IdTable, cycle detection.\" />","locale":"en"},"5991":{"id":"/en/docs/development-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"API design\" href=\"/docs/development-design\" description=\"Services and access, effects, plugins and configuration, events and logger.\" />","locale":"en"},"5992":{"id":"/en/docs/development-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"Loader internals\" href=\"/docs/development-loader\" description=\"kardia-loader, kardia-include, dynamic modules and HMR.\" />","locale":"en"},"5993":{"id":"/en/docs/development-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"Testing\" href=\"/docs/development-testing\" description=\"Ported TypeScript specs, model-based tests of the metatheory, tooling.\" />","locale":"en"},"5994":{"id":"/en/docs/development-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"Status and deviations\" href=\"/docs/development-status\" description=\"Implementation phases, status, and every departure from the plan.\" />","locale":"en"},"5995":{"id":"/en/docs/development-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"Risks\" href=\"/docs/development-risks\" description=\"Risks and open decisions, and a worked example of the API.\" />","locale":"en"},"5996":{"id":"/en/docs/development-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"Contributing\" href=\"/docs/development-contributing\" description=\"Building, testing, CI, releases and this documentation site.\" />","locale":"en"},"5997":{"id":"/en/docs/development-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development","content":"<Card title=\"Server architecture and acceptance\" href=\"/docs/development-server\" description=\"Ownership, recovery, upstream comparisons and native CI evidence.\" />","locale":"en"},"5998":{"id":"/en/docs/development-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#goals-list","content":"**Semantic fidelity to the calculus** (paper §4): fibers, target vs. committed views, the guard on unload, inertia, failure outcomes, single source per (key, realm), confluence.","locale":"en"},"5999":{"id":"/en/docs/development-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#goals-list","content":"**Behavioural parity with Cordis core** where the paper is silent (epoch with revision, two-level guard, concurrent fiber-level disposal, `update` veto, `replace`), using the TypeScript test suite as the acceptance specification.","locale":"en"},"6000":{"id":"/en/docs/development-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#goals-list","content":"**Idiomatic Rust**: typed service keys, explicit caller attribution instead of `Proxy`, `Send + Sync` handles usable from a multi-threaded tokio runtime, no `unsafe` in the core.","locale":"en"},"6001":{"id":"/en/docs/development-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#goals-list","content":"A declarative **loader** (entries, groups, realms) and file-backed **include** with two-way sync.","locale":"en"},"6002":{"id":"/en/docs/development-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#non-goals","content":"Re-evaluating plugin *code* at runtime (HMR). Rust has no module registry; this becomes an optional, later \"module source\" extension (Loader internals).","locale":"en"},"6003":{"id":"/en/docs/development-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#non-goals","content":"npm-specific tooling (`package-manager`, `create`, `loader-utils`). Not ported.","locale":"en"},"6004":{"id":"/en/docs/development-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#non-goals","content":"Byte-exact parity of error messages and UTF-16 column numbers (nice-to-have, not required).","locale":"en"},"6005":{"id":"/en/docs/development-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#non-goals","content":"HMR was a non-goal for the first releases only. It has since been implemented in `kardia-hmr` (WASM, dylib and process backends, watch mode); see Status and deviations.","locale":"en"},"6006":{"id":"/en/docs/development-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"A summary; each decision is justified on the page linked in the last column.","locale":"en"},"6007":{"id":"/en/docs/development-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"#","locale":"en"},"6008":{"id":"/en/docs/development-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"Decision","locale":"en"},"6009":{"id":"/en/docs/development-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"Section","locale":"en"},"6010":{"id":"/en/docs/development-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"D1","locale":"en"},"6011":{"id":"/en/docs/development-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development#decisions","content":"`Send + Sync` core; one state lock per root; **no user code runs while the lock is held**; every JS \"synchronous segment\" becomes one critical section; follow-up work is re