about a Cordis application as though it were statically assembled.\""],[360,"> “正是这条定理允许我们把一个 Cordis 应用当作静态装配的来推理。”"],[5148,"> For every sequence of effect functions a component applies, the accumulator recovers the context it began at (Theorem 7), and reverting the sequence hands each inverse the state its own application ran against (Theorem 16). Loading a component is running one iterator and accumulating its inverses in `φ`; unloading it is applying `φ`."],[595,"> 对组件所应用的任意效应函数序列，累加器都能恢复它开始时的上下文（定理 7），而撤销该序列时，每个逆操作拿到的都是其对应应用所面对的状态（定理 16）。加载组件就是运行一个迭代器并把它的逆操作累加到 `φ` 中；卸载组件就是应用 `φ`。"],[4259,"≺ 的无环性"],[4474,"0.1.0"],[4492,"0.1.0"],[4498,"0.1.0"],[9027,"0.1.0"],[9045,"0.1.0"],[9051,"0.1.0"],[4504,"0.3.2"],[9057,"0.3.2"],[1371,"1"],[5924,"1"],[1195,"1,180 个扩展名；26 组 Static 配置各 15 个路径；不归一化语义差异"],[4456,"1.0.0"],[4486,"1.0.0"],[9009,"1.0.0"],[9039,"1.0.0"],[4450,"1.0.0-rc.9"],[9003,"1.0.0-rc.9"],[4468,"1.1.2"],[9021,"1.1.2"],[4480,"1.1.4"],[9033,"1.1.4"],[4462,"1.2.0"],[9015,"1.2.0"],[3843,"100 ms"],[8396,"100 ms"],[5969,"15 timer tests: synchronous/asynchronous errors across all four schedulers, caller attribution and awaiting callbacks on disposal; repeated explicit instance failures under all three policies."],[5968,"17 core tests: repeated failure under all three policies, recovery after cleanup, independent plugin progress, default manual handling, failed loads, no unchanged validation retries, throwing/rejecting host handlers, and cancellation on policy changes or removal."],[1373,"2"],[5926,"2"],[4544,"224"],[9097,"224"],[5744,"224 on Linux/macOS, 226 on Windows: original 201 plus Proxy 10, Static 2 and FS 11 (two extra FS cases on Windows)"],[4548,"233"],[9101,"233"],[4532,"246"],[9085,"246"],[5746,"254 files at fixture SHA `7fc6d9ff7cc01987aa73006757792a5ca198023a` plus 2 supplements, covering all 171 MIME values"],[4540,"285"],[9093,"285"],[1375,"3"],[4457,"3"],[5928,"3"],[9010,"3"],[4524,"324"],[9077,"324"],[1377,"4"],[5930,"4"],[4444,"4.0.0-rc.12"],[8997,"4.0.0-rc.12"],[1379,"5"],[5932,"5"],[5970,"5 additional bridge tests: three manual/retry/host recovery rounds in a real Rust provider, JS consumer withdrawal/recreation, uninterrupted independent Rust/JS calls and timer progress without restarting the bridge, plus removal cancelling retry/host recovery."],[4528,"553"],[9081,"553"],[1381,"7"],[5934,"7"],[4520,"78"],[9073,"78"],[4536,"83"],[9089,"83"],[6484,"A **fiber** is one instantiation of a plugin. Instantiating a plugin registers, on the current fiber, an effect whose inverse retires the new fiber:"],[6989,"A **group** is an entry with `group: true` running the `group` plugin. Its `config` is a list of entries, which the group reconciles in place on every update instead of restarting: entries are created, updated and removed by id, concurrently, and one bad entry does not stop the others. Groups are never disabled themselves; their `disabled` verdict is passed on to their members."],[6893,"A **module** is identified by a `ModuleId`, an absolute path (`/app/plugins/foo.wasm`). Its code comes from a `ModuleSource`:"],[7399,"A **service** is a value that one plugin provides and other plugins depend on. The runtime tracks who provides what and who depends on what: a plugin that declares a service starts once the service is available, restarts when it is replaced, and stops when it goes away. This page shows how to define, provide and consume services, how calls are attributed to their caller, how isolation and interception change what a context sees, and how to use events and the logger."],[6990,"A **tree** is a set of groups with one backend that persists its changes. The loader's own tree lives in memory. A plugin can create a subtree (`loader.create_tree(ctx, backend)`); the include does this for each file."],[6971,"A `Catalog` is the static resolver: a registry of plugins by name."],[6472,"A `Disposal` is the completion of a disposal. It makes progress without being polled: the synchronous part has already run when `dispose()` returns, and any asynchronous rest runs as a spawned task, so dropping a `Disposal` does not cancel it. Await it to observe the result; `disposal.is_ready()` tells whether everything finished synchronously. Disposing an asynchronous effect whose setup is still running waits for the setup first."],[6421,"A `Disposer` is the inverse of an effect. It is consumed when it runs, so it runs at most once:"],[7316,"A `Failed` plugin clears its failure through `update` or an implementation replacement in the registry. Neither `restart()` nor a dependency refresh clears the error. `update_with(config, true)` can also recover it without emitting the `ConfigCommitted` event used for configuration write-back."],[6675,"A `GuestExit` has the exit `status`, its `cause` (`ExitCause::Exited` on its own, `Killed(reason)` for a missed deadline, a protocol violation or `kill()`, `Disposed` by its fiber) and the `stderr_tail` (16 KiB). stderr lines are also logged as the plugin at `debug`."],[6891,"A `ModuleGraph` is also a `Resolver`, so the loader can resolve entry names through it; `Hmr::plugin(graph)` is the `hmr` plugin over the same graph:"],[6492,"a `PluginDef`"],[7293,"A `PluginDef` and a JSON configuration"],[7258,"A `PluginDef` is the type-erased form the runtime stores. You can build one from a closure:"],[8071,"a `Promise<function>`"],[7220,"A `serde::Serialize` value that also has a JSON view"],[7420,"a `Serialize` value that also has a **JSON view**, readable with `ctx.view(key)`, by loader expressions and by guest plugins. A `serde_json::Value` provided under a key (`provide_as`, `provide_arc_as`, `provide_checked`) is its own JSON view."],[5881,"A `Service` comes back from `ctx.get` as a view whose `caller` is the accessing context (Kardia's `Svc::caller`). Views are `Proxy` objects, so a service's `#private` fields do not pass through them; use `private`."],[7410,"a `Service` under `S::NAME`"],[7214,"A `Service` value under its key"],[6655,"A `Spawner` starts the processes, so the app decides how a guest runs (a sandbox, a process group, a process manager that owns it):"],[6666,"a `tokio::sync::watch` receiver of the report (`None` while the process runs)"],[7082,"A `TreeBackend` decides where a tree's changes go. Every method has a default:"],[4831,"A binding counts as available only while the installing fiber is `ACTIVE`; a provider entering `UNLOADING` stops providing, so dependents recompute an unsatisfied target and begin their own teardown *while its bindings are still in place*."],[7604,"a binding of `key` in `realm` appears or disappears; delivered only to listeners whose context resolves `key` to that realm"],[7471,"A binding that a fiber on the way provides itself, in the same realm, is returned directly."],[4562,"A calculus of dynamic composition"],[5211,"A calculus of dynamic composition"],[4602,"A calculus with realms would relax disjointness to \"disjoint within a realm\" (§4.4, see Metatheory). The single shared realm restricts components with a non-empty provision to one fiber at a time."],[6638,"A call fails with `the guest instance is disposed` once the providing fiber is gone, with `the guest process exited` once a process guest has ended on its own, and with `invalid result of <key>.<method>: …` if the guest returns something that is not JSON. A guest calling back into an instance that is already running on the same thread (directly or through other guests) fails with `re-entrant call into a guest instance` instead of deadlocking. Like any service, a guest service is withdrawn when the guest's fiber is disposed."],[6121,"A Cargo workspace at the repository root, next to the untouched `cordis/` reference code (a local checkout of the TypeScript sources, ignored by git), `examples/`, `docs/` and the documentation site in `site/`:"],[8322,"A change from one non-⊥ target to another while `ACTIVE` goes through `UNLOADING` and then chains into `LOADING` (unload checks the epoch at its end)."],[5373,"A change to"],[7063,"a changed `name` or `inject`, or an entry that is not running: the entry is rebuilt (its fibers disposed, then started again);"],[6937,"A changed module does not load, or no longer exports a plugin"],[7166,"a char of an expression is located through YAML escapes;"],[7281,"A check or transform of the raw configuration before it is decoded"],[8317,"A child fiber is therefore exactly Definition 52: an ordinary effect of the parent whose inverse retires it. Unloading a parent cascades to its children through the parent's accumulator. Unlike the calculus, retirement and removal are fused: the inverse retires, waits for the chain to finish, and then the fiber is gone (its uid is already `null`)."],[8041,"a child plugin *inherits* the injection of an ancestor (`inject` declared on an ancestor admits access in descendants);"],[7707,"A client can close at any time, and the Rust app usually runs without any window open. So a host plugin should look a client service up when it needs it (`live_as`), rather than declare it in `inject`, which would stop that plugin whenever the window closes. The client provides its services again after every reconnect."],[7705,"A client can provide a service to the host, as an effect of the calling plugin:"],[7440,"a closure plugin"],[4697,"A coeffect at key `k` is a pair `(𝒱_k, 𝒜_k)`; each operation `a ∈ 𝒜_k` with argument `X_a` and outcome `B_a` acts **on the value alone**:"],[7235,"A compile error if `S` is not in `D`"],[7398,"A complete example"],[4923,"A component takes a configuration bound into its effect function; one component with two payloads is two components. The declarative layer may **revise** a running fiber (replace its config, reassign realms, disable or re-enable it). Disabling is O-Retire; every other revision is retire → deactivate → remove (children first) → reinsert at the same name. Re-enabling uses this composite rather than resetting `τ` to `⊥` (the monotonicity of `τ` underlies Theorem 80, and a re-enabled orphan would activate with its creator gone). In the implementation, re-enabling an *entry* instantiates a *fresh fiber*: the entry is the identity across revisions, the fiber the identity of one enablement. The shortcuts the loader takes (config handed to the component, a realm moved without reloading the provider) are justified by their *endpoint* (Theorem 80)."],[5139,"A component's loading is not one effect but a **sequence**."],[7619,"a configuration resolution hook (`ConfigHook`) for every fiber of the root: it gets the source configuration and the next stage, ultimately the plugin's own decoding. The loader uses it to evaluate expressions."],[4990,"A consumer is started only when every injected key is available; every provide/withdraw re-evaluates affected consumers."],[8040,"a consumer reads the **committed** impl, not the live store, so it keeps seeing its dependency during its own teardown (Theorem 70);"],[6738,"a crash brings down the host"],[6595,"a crashing guest fails only its own plugin"],[5390,"a crate whose sources or manifest changed since the last release;"],[7171,"A custom `TreeBackend` gets the same output by implementing `locate` and `locate_expr`; `TreeHandle::locate` and `TreeHandle::locate_expr` call them for a local id."],[4612,"A deactivation cannot be one step: a consumer torn down *because its provider is leaving* runs teardown code that may need that very provider (for example, returning connections to a pool). So the **decision** is separated from the **act**, and the act is guarded."],[6001,"A declarative **loader** (entries, groups, realms) and file-backed **include** with two-way sync."],[8030,"a declared **accessor** → its getter;"],[7319,"A decoding error names the failing path, such as `invalid config: … (at items.1)`."],[7377,"A delay table waits each entry in turn, and its end is the limit; `window` starts the count over, so a plugin that faults once a day is not spent after its third fault forever:"],[5034,"A dependency cyc