ry's own tasks; `.cargo/config.toml` makes them `cargo xtask <command>`."],[9063,"The `yakumo` build orchestrator (`yakumo.yml`: `yakumo`, `yakumo-tsc`, `yakumo-esbuild`, `yakumo-vitest`): `build` = esbuild bundles + `tsc` declarations; `test` = vitest; coverage variants."],[7148,"the absolute path of the file"],[7497,"the accessing `Context`"],[5142,"The accumulator becomes `φ ∘ g₁ ∘ … ∘ g_k`, which reverts in LIFO order (Theorem 16)."],[6115,"The accumulator is `FiberData::disposables: IdTable<Arc<EffectCell>>`; there is no `EffectId` type (`IdTable` hands out `u64` ids)."],[8975,"The analysed code is the TypeScript checkout at `cordis/` (a separate git repository, ignored by the outer repository's `.gitignore`), at HEAD `6663348 fix(fs-node): reject drive-relative paths on Windows (#285)`. The `cordis` core package is version `4.0.0-rc.12`, the paper's \"Cordis v4\". File:line references (`reflect.ts:182-185`) are against that revision."],[7699,"The app creates a `Bridge`, mounts its plugin, and routes client requests into `Bridge::handle`. Plugins inject `bridge` and expose services; an exposure is an effect of the exposing plugin, so it is withdrawn when that plugin stops:"],[8932,"the associated property (e.g. service `foo.bar` reachable as `ctx.foo.bar`; mixins into associated types such as sessions)"],[6854,"The backend follows the file: `.wasm` or `.wat` runs on wasmtime, a file with the executable bit runs as a child process, and anything else loads as a dynamic library. `--replace <file>` moves that file over the guest's own path and hot-reloads it through HMR in the same run, so the reload path is observable too."],[6677,"The backends implement two public traits, which a host can implement for a transport of its own:"],[5449,"The base path is only for GitHub Pages, which serves the site under `/kardia/`; without `VITE_BASE_PATH`, the build serves from `/`."],[6324,"The binary starts `fs` and `fs:local` itself and includes the config file with `watch: true`, so the file is an `include` whose entries form the app."],[7222,"The binding is withdrawn when the plugin stops. Its consumers stop first."],[5516,"The boundaries are synchronous in the code (`scope.yield_(..)?`, `ctx.checkpoint()?`). `ctx.checkpoint()` fails once the load has been superseded (the fiber's target no longer equals the target of its episode) or the fiber was retired; `scope.checkpoint()` fails once the effect has been disposed. Async effect bodies are spawned and never dropped half-way; disposal waits for the body to reach its end or a boundary."],[5010,"The boundary divides the environment into locations **inside** (the system can modify them exclusively *and* restore them, so they are tracked and revertible) and **outside** (either ability fails, so the operation acts as `id_Γ` and is untracked)."],[7648,"The bridge"],[6676,"The bridge emits two events on the guest's fiber: `GuestStarted { fiber, handle }` once the process has started, before `apply`, and `GuestExited { fiber, exit }` when it ends before the fiber disposes it. The fiber then fails with the report as its error (`Fiber::fail`), which withdraws its services, so their consumers wait for them again. As for any failed fiber, `restart` keeps it failed; `fiber.update(config)` starts a new process."],[5900,"The bridge's calls are asynchronous, unlike the guest ABI's (Dynamic modules): it is the asynchronous contract of paper §6.2. Its end-to-end tests drive the real Rust host from the TypeScript client."],[7665,"The bridge's transport over one Tauri command and a `Channel`"],[5017,"The broker enables **load balancing** (policy-driven dispatch; scale by loading and unloading providers), **rolling updates** (load the new provider, shift traffic, unload the old one once drained: quiescence/tranquility), and **cross-process invocation** (each process hosts its own context; RPC preserves the interface; it must be designed against an *asynchronous* contract)."],[7927,"The browser build delegate