ovider reactivates only the dependents whose resolved dependency changed; a plugin with an unavailable dependency waits without erroring."],[6860,"There is no `cordis_call`, as the plugin provides no service."],[5440,"There is no `NPM_TOKEN`. If trusted publishing ever has to be bypassed, a granular npm access token limited to the `@kardiajs` packages, stored as an `npm` environment secret, would be the fallback; the workflow does not use one."],[7640,"There is no `tracing` bridge; the plan leaves a `tracing_subscriber::Layer` feeding exporters as an optional addition to `kardia-logger-console`."],[7429,"There is no in-place overwrite of a bound value. To change what consumers see, provide a new value (withdraw and re-provide, restart or update the provider), so that the change is observed."],[8401,"There is no loader-specific code: the loader imports plugins through the same internal ESM loader, so their modules are in the cache hmr inspects. hmr replaces plugins at the **registry** level, and loader entries keep their fibers."],[7341,"These features are on the source main branch; this work did not publish a new crates.io release. Use source containing the Server acceptance commit. An older binary installed with `cargo install kardia` need not contain them."],[7684,"These new APIs are included in npm version 0.2.0. Rust and JS supervise their own plugin instances. A Rust recovery setting does not configure a JS plugin's policy."],[7907,"These packages are not described in the paper; they show how ordinary services are built on the core primitives."],[8974,"These pages describe Cordis, not kardia. Names such as `Context`, `Fiber`, `ctx.effect` and paths such as `cordis/packages/core/src/fiber.ts` refer to the TypeScript code. How kardia maps them to Rust is covered in the Development section."],[5959,"These tests check the implementation against the theory with no business logic copied into the tests."],[8701,"Things the paper assumes that the code does not check"],[7880,"Things to try while it runs"],[7715,"Third-party UI code runs with the webview's full access to Tauri: load only trusted code into the main webview, and give untrusted extensions declarative contributions or a sandboxed webview."],[8012,"This is Algorithm 3. The *filter* parameter exists so that the loader can replace the realm test during realm reassignment (Algorithm 7, see Managed realms)."],[5043,"This is an open problem."],[8047,"This is exactly `σ(k)(d(k) ⊕ ι(k))` from Definition 27, with ⊕ = shallow object merge (customizable per service). The `logger` service uses it for `{ name, level }` (`logger.ts:213-224`)."],[8945,"This is how independent plugins extend each other's types at runtime: the dynamic counterpart of TypeScript module augmentation (paper §6.4, see Discussion and related work)."],[7675,"This is the arrangement the paper describes for crossing a process boundary (each process hosts its own context, and calls between them follow an asynchronous contract, see Related work)."],[5515,"This is the loader's outer-fiber pattern (`yield () => {}` after import) made explicit."],[4599,"This is well defined because `dom(σ_n) ⊆ p_n` and the provisions of distinct fibers are disjoint (O-Insert enforces it). Each key thus has at most one possible provider, `provider_k(γ)`."],[5199,"this page"],[8704,"This page compares the theory (the paper's model and its pseudo-code, §3–5; see Concepts) with the implementation (the TypeScript code at `cordis/`). It is the bridge used by the Rust design: where the two differ, the Development pages state which one kardia follows and why."],[7970,"This page covers `cordis/packages/core/src/context.ts`, `reflect.ts` and `utils.ts`. The paper counterparts are §3.2 (coeffects, see Reactive coeffects), §5.1.2 (Algorithms 2–3) and §5.1.4 (Algorithm 6), both in Implementation."],[8136,"This page covers `cordis/packages/core/src/events.ts` and `logger.ts`. In the paper, event listeners are the canonical *commutative key* (\"registration takes an entry of its own\", §3.4.2, see The context paradigm); the logger is an example of interception metadata."],[8062,"This page covers `cordis/packages/core/src/fiber.ts:60-363` and `utils.ts`. The paper counterpart is §3.1 and Algorithm 1; see Revertible effects."],[8228,"This page covers `cordis/packages/core/src/fiber.ts` and `registry.ts`. The paper counterparts are §4.1–4.2 (the calculus, see Calculus) and §5.1.3 (Algorithms 4–5, see Implementation)."],[8902,"This page covers `cordis/packages/core/src/service.ts` and `utils.ts:113-238`. The paper counterparts are §5.1.4 (context access, see Implementation) and §6.3 (capabilities, see Discussion and related work). The mechanism is mostly *implicit* in the paper, but it is essential for temporal composability in practice."],[8380,"This page covers `cordis/packages/hmr/src/index.ts` and `error.ts`, plus `core/src/registry.ts:185-209` and `core/src/fiber.ts:401-416` (see `registry.replace`). The paper counterpart is §5.2.2, Algorithms 8–10; see Implementation."],[8464,"This page covers `cordis/packages/include/src/{index,journal,patch}.ts` and `packages/group/src/index.ts`. The paper counterpart is §5.2.1 (\"`@cordisjs/include` loads an external configuration file (YAML or JSON) and grafts its entries in as a nested subtree\", see Implementation). The paper does not describe the synchronization machinery below; it is an implementation contribution."],[8550,"This page covers `cordis/packages/loader/src/**`. The paper counterparts are §5.2.1 *Declarative configuration* (see Implementation) and §4.4 *Configuration* (see Metatheory). File:line references on this page are relative to `packages/loader/src/`."],[5473,"This page covers the public API of `kardia-core` and the built-in plugin crates: services and access (decisions D3, D4), effects (D5, D6), plugins, configuration and the lifecycle (D7), and events, the logger and built-in services. The data structures these operate on are on Core data structures. For a user-oriented introduction, see the guide pages Plugins, Services and Effects."],[5285,"This page is decision **D1** of the guiding decisions: a `Send + Sync` core with one state lock per root, in which no user code runs while the lock is held."],[6968,"This page is the library user's view: how to set the loader up, drive it from Rust, include files and find config errors. The entry fields themselves (`id`, `name`, `config`, `group`, `disabled`, `inject`, `intercept`, `isolate`) and expressions are listed in The configuration file. How the loader is built is on Loader internals, and every place where it departs from the TypeScript loader is listed on Status and deviations."],[4760,"This page records what the *paper* (arXiv:2608.25512, §5) says Cordis does: Algorithms 1–10 and Table 2. It sits between theory and design. The actual TypeScript code differs in several details; those differences are catalogued on Paper vs. code, and the code itself is described in the Cordis reference."],[6411,"This page shows how to register effects, how they are disposed and what a fiber's handle can do. For the theory see Revertible effects; for the design decisions (non-owning handles, inertial loads instead of dropped futures) see API design; for the state machine underneath see Core data structures."],[5109,"This page summarises §3.1 of the paper (arXiv:2608.25512). Definition and theorem numbers are the paper's. For how Cordis implements effects, see Effects in the Cordis reference; for kardia's Rust design, see Effects in the guide and API design."],[4960,"This page summarises §3.2 of the paper (arXiv:2608.25512). Definition numbers are the paper's. For the Cordis realisation see Context and reflection and Fiber lifecycle in the Cordis reference."],[4695,"This page summarises §3.3–3.4 of the paper (arXiv:2608.25512). It is the part of the paper that turns *local* guarantees (one component, see Revertible effects and Reactive coeffects) into the precondition for *global* guarantees (many interleaved components, §4.3, see Metatheory)."],[4579,"This page summarises §4.1–4.2 of the paper (arXiv:2608.25512). It is the reference for the *state machine* the Rust core must implement. For how Cordis realises it, see Fiber lifecycle in the Cordis reference; for kardia's state machine, see Lifecycle."],[4884,"This page summarises §4.3–4.4 of the paper (arXiv:2608.25512). Each theorem is stated informally, with the practical guarantee it buys and the implementation obligation it implies. The rules it reasons about are on the calculus page."],[5009,"This page summarises §6–8 of the paper (arXiv:2608.25512): theory and design guidance. The paper's notes that bear on a Rust port are marked **\\[Rust]** in the source summary; here each is followed by an \"In kardia\" callout saying how kardia realises it."],[8839,"This reference targets Cordis 6ad12cf2: core, ACL, Echo, Static and Proxy are all 2.0.0-rc.0. Expectations do not follow a floating main branch or npm version."],[5577,"This replaces JS prototype chains (`Object.create`, `setPrototypeOf`, `swap`). Own vs. inherited is explicit."],[5174,"This section does not describe the Cordis code base (see the Cordis reference) or kardia's Rust design (see Development). Where a concept has a direct counterpart in kardia, an \"In kardia\" callout says so and links the relevant page."],[5984,"This section is the design of kardia, the Rust re-implementation of the Cordis framework. It builds on"],[5441,"This site lives in `site/`: Fumadocs on TanStack Start (React), built with Vite and Tailwind CSS, with Bun as the package manager and script runner. It is outside the Cargo workspace, so `cargo` commands are unaffected."],[1389,"Thm 64"],[5942,"Thm 64"],[1392,"Thm 68 / Cor 69"],[5945,"Thm 68 / Cor 69"],[1395,"Thm 70"],[5948,"Thm 70"],[1398,"Thm 71"],[5951,"Thm 71"],[1401,"Thm 73"],[5954,"Thm 73"],[4945,"Thm 73 assumption, §6.5 (Discussion)"],[1404,"Thm 80"],[5957,"Thm 80"],[5658,"Threading model"],[483